\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares On The Mend For Now \b0\par\par\uc2 2,315 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 06:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Treasurys A Bit Lighter, But Still Backed\par DJIA          7891.08    gain 6.09      up 0.1%\par NASDAQ        1337.52    gain 13.58     up 1.0%\par S&P 500       841.15     gain 3.87      up 0.5%\par Dow Future    7963.00    gain 43.00     up 0.5%\par NASDAQ Future 1020.00    gain 9.50      up 0.9%\par S&P Future    849.00     gain 8.10      up 1.0%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.70% down 0.05\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Stock investors vacillated between jitters about a possible war with Iraq and optimism over upbeat economic news for much of the day Friday, finally sending the market higher in the final minutes of trading. Treasury prices came off slightly in Tokyo trade, having finished the weekend higher in New York. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Despite Friday's gains, analysts remain dubious of Wall Street's ability to forge an upward path in the face of uncertainty over Iraq. And, despite Friday's advances, the major indexes ended the week lower, snapping a two-week winning stretch. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there are any bits of good news sufficient enough to really drive the market higher on a sustained basis," said Richard A. Dickson, senior market strategist at Lowry's Research Reports in Palm Beach, Fla. \par \par \uc2  A top member of Turkey's governing party rejected a new vote on letting U.S. troops use the country to open a northern front against Iraq - a second blow in Turkey to U.S. war planners in as many days. \par \par \uc2  The United Arab Emirates won support Sunday from two other Persian Gulf nations in its call for Saddam Hussein to quit power to avert a war, while Iraq poured scorn on the Emirates, calling it a tool of Israel. \par \par \uc2  Arab leaders Saturday refused to discuss the proposal, which was the first open call by an Arab nation for Saddam to go into exile. \par \par \uc2  And China has been "working unswervingly" toward a peaceful solution to the situation in Iraq and remains adamantly opposed to war, the government's official Xinhua News Agency said Monday in an exceptionally long report detailing the country's efforts. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: U.S. Treasurys saw light profit-taking in Tokyo trading Monday after a sharp rally on Wall Street Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Prices fell slightly due to profit-taking after the gains in New York. Some Japanese banks appeared to have sold short-end papers. But trade was thin ahead of the ISM data today," a senior bond trader at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Institute for Supply Management will release its U.S. February manufacturing index Monday, and the U.S. Labor Department will release February jobs data Friday. \par \par \uc2  Forecasters expect the February ISM index to stand at 52 versus 53.9 in January. Such a reading would indicate a modest slowdown in overall factory sector activity. \par \par \uc2  The Treasury market is still supported by bargain-hunting on price dips amid ongoing bullish sentiment, some said. \par \par \uc2  Traders speculated there is limited room for Treasury prices to rise from current high levels, but remain negative about actively selling Treasurys because of fragile U.S. stock prices. \par \par \uc2  Another trader said there is little room for short-end paper to rise further but some hope the yield curve in medium- and long-end papers will flatten further if U.S. stock prices stay weak. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Shares Lifted By Weaker Yen\par USD-Yen             118.02       loss  0.20    dn 0.2%\par AUD-USD             0.6068       loss  0.0010  dn 0.2%\par Nikkei 225          8467.05      gain  104.51  up 1.2%\par Hang Seng           9312.26      gain  189.60  up 2.1%\par JGB Yield           0.7850% up 0.0050\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks edged higher Monday morning, buoyed by\par Wall Street's gains and the dollar's strength against the Japanese yen\par last Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stock prices were supported by buying from exporters who welcomed the\par yen's slip against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Export-dependent high-tech stocks, such as electronics giants Fujitsu,\par NEC and Sony were among morning gainers, while banks including UFJ and\par Mizuho fell.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said Monday that North Korean\par leader Kim Jong Il has broken some of the promises of the Pyongyang\par Declaration, which they signed last year to improve regional security,\par Kyodo News reported.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Pyongyang Declaration has been breached in some parts," Koizumi\par said during a parliamentary session, commenting on recent reports of\par North Korea firing a missile and reactivating a nuclear reactor.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Shares Expected To Improve Slightly\par Euro-USD            1.0788      gain  0.0011  up 0.1%\par Stlg-USD            1.5758      gain  0.0030  up 0.2%\par USD-Franc           1.3558      loss  0.0002  dn 0.01%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European stock prices should rise slightly at the\par opening, with government bonds and the euro holding steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares should open slightly higher Monday on news\par that a key al-Qaeda figure was captured and the perception that the\par publicity momemtum has tipped again toward Saddam Hussein after he\par destroyed some disputed missiles.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks were higher Friday as a triple dose of\par better-than-expected U.S. economic data raised hopes that the outlook\par for global growth in 2003 may be sunnier than previously anticipated.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to Tom Hougaard at City Index: "There are a majority of\par institutional investors in the U.S. who believe that once war breaks\par out, the stock market will rally. On the other side of the camp are the\par bears saying that the war has nothing to do with the weakness in the\par market. It is the economic fundamentals that are key."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds should open little changed as the\par market awaits further developments on Iraq and North Korea. Meanwhile,\par hopes for an ECB cut this week will underpin the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bond prices rose Friday, with the yield curve\par steepening.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market extended gains as comments from ECB officials fueled rate\par cut expectations for next week," said Nathalie Fillet, fixed-income\par strategist with BNP Paribas.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Economic data were mixed, but recent comments from the Bush\par administration on the destruction by Iraq of its missiles wasn't enough\par supported bond prices," said Fillet.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Even a possible rate cut from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par unlikely to push the euro out of its stubborn range against the dollar\par this week. The overarching theme of war precedes more traditional\par concerns such as the health of the global economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, follow-through buying of the dollar against the yen is\par expected after Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data confirmed Friday that the BOJ had\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in late February to weaken the\par yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Much like last week, market direction will mirror the ping-pong\par movements of international diplomacy. Uncertainty surrounding the timing\par and length of a possible U.S.-led war against Iraq discourages the\par dollar and its major rivals from exploring new territory.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This period of uncertainty around Iraq has frozen everyone," said\par John McCarthy, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Barings Capital\par Markets. "There have been some interesting developments and certainly\par the decision by Hussein to destroy the missiles is seen in some respects\par as positive but it doesn't clarify the ultimate outcome."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While war rhetoric from all the key players threatens to make for very\par choppy trading, McCarthy said the euro, which has been stuck between\par $1.0730 and $1.0850, will "stay in this range until there is a\par resolution in Iraq."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Monday, March 3: U.K., U.S. Manufacturing\par ET/GMT\par 0430/0930 U.K.         February Manufacturing Purchasing\par 0430/0930 U.K.         Final January M4 Lending\par 0430/0930 U.K.         Final January M4 Money Supply\par 0430/0930 U.K.         January Consumer Lending\par 0430/0930 U.K.         Provisional M0 Money Supply\par 0830/1330 U.S.         January Personal Income (last +0.4%)\par 0830/1330 U.S.         January Personal Spending (last +0.9%)\par 0900/1400 France       Weekly T-bill Auction results\par 0945/1445 U.S.         Federal Reserve Gov Bies addresses\par 1000/1500 U.S.         February Manufacturing Index\par 1000/1500 U.S.         January Construction Spending\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KPN (KPN): FY Earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR9.6B (EUR7.5B)\par Consensus sales: EUR12.1B (EUR12.36B)\par Note: Analysts see small EUR30M profit in 4Q, but this will be dwarfed\par by FY loss mainly on writedown of 3G goodwill, licenses earlier in\par '02. Eyes on whether company achieves financial '02 targets set\par earlier.\par HSBC Holdings (HBC): FY earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 9 analysts): US$9.79B (US$8B)\par Forecast range: US$9.25B - US$10.1B\par Note: This year, the focus is expected to be on the progress of HSBC's\par acquisition of Household International, which is expected to be\par completed next month.\par GKN (U.GKN): FY earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP251M (GBP245M)\par Consensus sales: GBP4.28B (GBP4.34B)\par Note: Focus on plans of new CEO and pension deficit, with additional\par '03 contributions seen at GBP10M-GBP15M. Assuming no surprises in\par outlook, Mon's US auto statistics seen as greater influence on shares.\par Pearson (PSO): FY earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 9 analysts): GBP390M (GBP294M)\par Consensus sales: GBP4.25B (GBP4.25B)\par Note: Investors will seek update on US schools education sales amid\par fears of government budget cutbacks in the event of a war. Will also\par want update on outlook for Financial Times advertising and future\par plans for FT, which is due for a relaunch.\par Wanadoo (F.WDO): FY Earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 12 analysts): EUR38M (EUR193M)\par Note: Focus on EBITDA, French directories' margin and performance of\par Freeserve and Spanish assets, says London analyst. Adds will also look\par at 03 business results outlook and possible strategy change after\par arrival of new CEO and CFO.\par Inchcape (U.INC): FY earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (Multex, 5 analysts): GBP108.4M (GBP97.9M)\par Forecast range: GBP106M - GBP110M\par Consensus sales: GBP3.28B (GBP3.23B)\par Note: The automotive services group said in December that its full-year\par results would beat expectations.\par Equant (ENT): F