\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips as market eyes U.S. factory data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 19:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 3 (Reuters) - The dollar eased on Monday as listless U.S. manufacturing data encouraged selling of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but dealers said they expected it to remain in well-established ranges absent major developments in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "Manufacturing is still growing, but it's a real wait-and-see situation. There's a lot of geopolitical risk, not just in Iraq, but with oil prices up from the strike in Venezuela and North Korea not helping," said Kurt Karl, chief economist at Swiss Re America in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By midday in New York, the dollar was down about a third of a percent\par against the euro at $1.0828 per euro  and off four tenths of a percent\par against the Swiss franc at 1.3467 francs       .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Joe Francomano, vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Erste Bank in New\par York, said the uncertainty over what will happen in Iraq, and how that\par will affect the U.S. economy, was keeping pressure on the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People seem to be writing off the first half of the year, whether it's\par because of the mixed U.S. economic data, high oil prices or worry over\par Iraq. Other than the housing market, we're not seeing much sign of\par growth," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's tough to make gains in market conditions like that, and obviously\par we are seeing confidence in the dollar erode," Francomano added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A key survey on manufacturing in the United States showed the\par manufacturing sector expanded for a fourth straight month but at a\par slower-than-expected pace.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Institute for Supply Management said its February index slipped to\par 50.5 in February from 53.9 a month earlier, below expectations of a dip\par to 52.4. It said concerns about a possible war with Iraq were a "major\par deterrent" for many industries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar eased 0.23 percent to 117.85\par yen .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said they were reluctant to sell the dollar aggressively\par against the yen after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  disclosed last week that it had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in February for a second straight month to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par IRAQ THE KEY\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The weak U.S. factory data also put pressure on U.S. stocks, which had\par opened higher on speculation that recent events may have pushed back a\par possible war on Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Baghdad agreed over the weekend to destroy banned missiles and on\par Monday said it would submit a report on its stocks of biological\par weapons.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Turkey's parliament over the weekend blocked a U.S. plan to use the\par country as a base for a possible attack on Iraq, though on Monday the\par two parties said they were reviewing other options.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Equity investors also were cheered by news that a key member of the al\par Qaeda network had been captured in Pakistan, an arrest U.S. officials\par said was significant in the war on terror.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Oil and gold prices also were lower on easing war worries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers were more cautious, with some reading Turkey's\par surprise rejection of U.S. troops as a further blow to White House\par efforts at coalition building, and therefore a negative for the\par dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The worst-case scenario for the dollar would be for the United States\par to wage war on Iraq with limited international backing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The dollar has been range trading, and will continue to do, probably\par with a weaker bias, until we some resolution on the Iraq issue," said\par John McCarthy, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Capital Markets in\par New York.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MEETINGS AWAITED\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar showed little reaction to an unexpected 0.1 percent fall in\par U.S. consumer spending, long a mainstay of the economic recovery.\par Economists had expected a 0.1 percent rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It also shrugged off a bigger-than-expected 1.7 percent rise in\par construction spending in January.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Data from Europe were mixed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Switzerland's industrial sector contracted further, with a key\par purchasing management index falling to 48 in February from 48.3 in\par January, the eighth time in nine months that the index has been below\par the boom-or-bust line of 50.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But manufacturing in the euro zone stabilized in February. The Reuters\par Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index rose to 50.1 last month from 49.3\par in January, beating consensus forecasts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Nevertheless, analysts said the survey was unlikely to stop the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cutting interest rates this week to shore up\par business and consumer confidence.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Reuters poll taken last week found that 38 of 53 economists expect\par the ECB to cut rates this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Erste Bank's Francomano, echoing a sentiment expressed by other\par traders, said an ECB rate cut would erode the euro's yield advantage,\par "which may favor the dollar."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Central banks of Japan, Britain, Australia, New Zealand, Canada and\par Norway also hold policy meetings this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Norway is widely expected to cut rates and economists believe there is\par some chance that Canada will raise rates. But the other banks are\par expected to keep interest rates on hold.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030303dz3300xno\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
