\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar saved by  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2 , charts gloomy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 09:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 3 (Reuters) - If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February, the dollar would be two to three yen below where it is now, testing seven-month lows, technical analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  But it is too early for the Japanese authorities to rejoice at having protected a key level around 116.80 yen, a low reached in September, and their fight to keep the yen from rising to protect Japan's export-led recovery is far from won. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is looking bearish," said Takeshi Iio, vice president at UFJ Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing section. "It's scary to think what might happen if it fails to hold above 116.85 yen." \par \par \uc2  Hurt by mounting fears of a war in Iraq and signs of weakness in the U.S. economy, the dollar fell to a six-month low of 116.85 yen last Thursday before pushing back up, which traders said was probably the result of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry confirmed on Friday that it had asked the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  a few times in late February. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed Japan spent about 513 billion yen, equivalent to $4.34 billion at Monday's rate, in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February, adding to the 678 billion yen spent in January. \par \par \uc2  "The market thinks the dollar could fall fast should it fall under that level around 116.80," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at JP Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  Traders said heavy option-related selling was likely should the dollar fall under key levels such as 116.82, August's low of 116.30 and the 115.50 yen reached in July. Many players expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in before such levels are hit. \par \par \uc2  The new head of MOF's international bureau, Hiroshi Watanabe, has said he is an avid watcher of technical charts and that it is important to prevent rates from reaching critical levels. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar could've lost another two or three yen if it wasn't for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said UFJ's Iio. \par \par \uc2  CLOUD OVER THE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  But the battle is not over. \par \par \uc2  "With the Ichi Moku cloud above, and the 20-day moving average around 119 yen, the dollar will be weighed down," said JP Morgan's Tanase. \par \par \uc2  A cloud on the Ichi Moku Kinko Hyo, developed by a Japanese journalist in 1935, is an enclosed area between two key lines on a graph and is often taken as a signal of support or resistance, depending on the direction of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movement. \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed to break above the cloud and was pushed back out after a brief upward attack in February. \par \par \uc2  As of Monday, the bottom end of the Ichi Moku cloud was at around 118.71 yen and the 20-day moving average at 119.25 yen. The dollar was hovering around 118.08 yen in late Asian trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also cited resistance around 118.70 with a key 38.2 Fibonacci retracement from the dollar's low of 116.85 and a recent high of 121.78 reached February 11 seen around that level. \par \par \uc2  Fibonacci levels are derived from a number sequence found by 13th century Italian mathematician Leonardo Fibonacci, which chartists believe can be applied to price behaviour. \par \par \uc2  "Both the Ichi Moku and the 38.2 (percent) retracement are seen around 118.70 so the dollar's rise is likely to slow down around that level," said JP Morgan's Tanase. ($1=118.18 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030303dz3300gj6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
