\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar softens as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  'fear effect' fades. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2003\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 3 (Reuters) - The dollar retreated from a one-week peak against the yen by late afternoon in Asia on Monday, with the "fear effect" from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's revelation that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  beginning to wear off. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supporting the market only psychologically," said Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley Japan. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had edged up to a session high of 118.30 yen, adding to gains made after Friday's confirmation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in February "by stealth" for a second straight month, which led many traders to conclude Japan will go back into the market again if the dollar threatens to fall. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback failed to sustain the gains for long and retreated to as low as 118.00 yen by late afternoon, although still up from Thursday's low of 117.02 yen in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself is not supposed to push up the dollar decisively anyway," said Umemoto. "Its purpose is to absorb speculative yen purchases when the yen is rising fast and not weaken the yen artificially." \par \par \uc2  At 0639 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 118.03/08 yen down slightly from 118.09/17 yen in late Friday U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  "We can't make much profits from going long on the dollar just because of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We need more factors to buy the dollar," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Most participants expect the dollar to stay in a tight band against the yen with its downside supported by possible further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities and rises inhibited by geopolitical risks. \par \par \uc2  HOW EFFECTIVE? \par \par \uc2  The euro was also easier versus the dollar at $1.0780/85 compared with $1.0800/06 in U.S. trade late last week. \par \par \uc2  It slipped to 127.24/41 yen from 127.62 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and dealers expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's strength due to worries that it could harm exports, the main pillar of an economy hobbled by years of deflation. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it had bought both euros and dollars for yen, although the euro-buying had been "far less". \par \par \uc2  "It was a bit of a surprise that they also bought the euro. I think it showed Japan was intent to stop the yen's overall rise, not just its rise against the dollar," said Kikuko Takeda, research manager at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "Their latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , conducted after the G7 meeting in late February, also suggests other G7 countries are not opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So that is a relief for the dollar," Takeda said. \par \par \uc2  Japan spent about 513 billion yen ($4.34 billion)in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February, compared with 678 billion yen ($5.74 billion) in January, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  data released on Friday showed. \par \par \uc2  But traders suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday and Friday last week, possibly bringing the total amount of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February close to 1.0 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  "When considering that (Japan) used to spend three to four trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has much more room to spend," said Mitsuru Sahara vice president of UFJ Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group. \par \par \uc2  "The actual February figure was probably larger as the figure doesn't include the last two days of the month, and this shows that Japan is absorbing most of of its monthly current account surplus (of around one trillion yen)," Sahara said. \par \par \uc2  WAR SEEN INEVITABLE \par \par \uc2  The greenback was still haunted by chances of a U.S.-led war against Iraq, a factor complicated by the Turkish Parliament's refusal to allow the deployment of U.S. troops in its territory near the Iraqi border. \par \par \uc2  The market showed little reaction to Saturday's decision by Turkey, which dealt a blow to U.S. planning for a possible war against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market would be unable to move much given huge uncertainty over Iraq ahead of Friday, when chief U.N. arms inspector Hans Blix is expected to make another report to the U.N. Security Council. \par \par \uc2  The United States and Britain are expected to seek U.N backing for war in the form of a resolution by the Security Council in the days after the Blix report, but it is unclear if they have enough support in the 15-member council. \par \par \uc2  For the immediate future, traders are looking to key U.S. data on manufacturers, due at 1500 GMT on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Institute of Supply Management index is expected to show a retreat to 52.4 for February, down from January's 53.9, but still above the 50 mark separating expansion and contraction. \par \par \uc2  Bulls hope a stronger reading could help the dollar but bears are sceptical given that on Friday the dollar failed to capitalise on either better-than-expected fourth-quarter U.S. gross domestic product data or a rise in February's University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey. ($1=118.18 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030303dz3300ei9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
