\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Climbs Amid Suspicion of BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry and Agnes T. Crane \par \uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2003\par 02:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar edged higher against its major rivals, consolidating gains as suspicions intensified that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also boosted by some stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data as well as a 1% rise in the Dow Jones Industrial Average. \par \par \uc2  Durable-goods orders rose 3.3% in January, the largest increase in six months, which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said certainly helped refocus the market's attention on some signs of strength in the U.S. economy, even though the market remains highly attuned to developments surrounding a potential U.S.-led war against Iraq and broader terrorist concerns. \par \par \uc2  On this front, the dollar got a steady albeit unspectacular boost from the Bush administration's decision to lower the national terror alert to yellow from orange. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday in New York, the dollar was at &#165;117.56, well off its &#165;116.86 intraday low and up from &#165;117.10 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.0760, well below its session high of $1.0836 and moderately lower than $1.0785 late Wednesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3596 francs, moderately up from 1.3556 francs late Wednesday. Sterling was at $1.5803, modestly lower than $1.5824 late Wednesday. It was against the yen that the dollar showed most strength,  confirming suspicions for some traders that the Japanese government had placed orders to buy dollars to encourage a weakening in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, reached Thursday night in Tokyo, declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  The debate, however, is likely to be moot Friday when the Bank of Japan is slated to release statistics that should show if the ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past month in an effort to push the yen lower. If the data, however, fail to provide any evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tinkering, the market is likely to be surprised. \par \par \uc2  In other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the South African rand was weaker against the dollar after Finance Minister Trevor Manuel reportedly late Wednesday called the rand overvalued at current levels. Late in New York, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 7.9936  rands to the dollar, weaker than late Wednesday's 8.1699 rands. \par \par \uc2  -- Adam Aljewicz in Johannesburg, South Africa, contributed to this article. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com and Agnes  T. Crane at agnes.crane@dowjones.com. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  02-27-03 2000ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030228dz2s000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
