\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Tokyo's admission leads to yen submission. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 784 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2003\par 23:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 28 (Reuters) - Japanese finance authorities on Friday confessed to the open secret of selling yen to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising, and their admission was rewarded as the yen fell against the dollar and euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was unable to capitalize broadly on better-than-expected fourth-quarter U.S. gross domestic product data, nor a rise in February's University of Michigan's consumer sentiment survey. \par \par \uc2  Traders chose not to test Tokyo's resolve to keep the yen from strengthening, viewing the effort warily. A strong yen puts a crimp in Japan's export sector, the lone area of strength for an economy suffering from deflation. \par \par \uc2  In its monthly report, the Bank of Japan revealed what many in the market had already suspected. Acting on behalf of the Finance Ministry, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, spending 513 billion yen ($4.36 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the Japanese unit's stubborn strength. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the market might be surprised that the total expenditure was not as much as speculated, which in turn could give Tokyo comfort that it succeeded in stopping the yen's slide without having to match market expectations. \par \par \uc2  "Japan could be relieved that people saw how much it spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sold the yen on it," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  Additionally, a report late on Friday showed speculative traders inflated their bets that the yen would continue to strengthen by boosting the number of net long yen futures contracts to their highest level since November 1999. \par \par \uc2  However, that positioning could lead to a rapid turnaround if the speculative traders sense a shift in the winds and start selling yen. That could put a wind into the sails of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  maneuvers. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to a session high 118.27 yen  before slipping to 118.10 yen, a gain of 0.45 percent from Thursday's New York close. On Thursday the dollar fell to a six-month low of 116.80 yen, where stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was thought to have put a floor underneath the greenback, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The euro soared to 127.62 yen  but edged back to 127.54 yen, a gain of 0.90 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  While the market was occupied with the yen for much of Friday, attention was still focused largely on the potential for war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "Until we come to the point of some sort of conflict, the massive unknown sits out there," keeping the dollar under pressure against all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to $1.08 , a gain of 0.45 percent on the day, and just below the session high $1.0807. \par \par \uc2  Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded against the dollar. The greenback fell to a 2-1/2 year low against the Canadian unit, striking C$1.4832 , a loss of 0.68 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar rose a similar amount against the U.S. dollar, trading at 60.86 cents . \par \par \uc2  DATA AND RATES, A LOOK AHEAD \par \par \uc2  Next week investors will be faced with a plethora of economic data and important \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings where interest rate policies could be changed. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll showed 20 of 22 economists expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to cut its benchmark interest rate from 2.75 percent on March 6. \par \par \uc2  Another poll shows 20 economists surveyed expect the Bank of England to leave interest rates unchanged at 3.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  The consensus among economists with regard to the Bank of Canada is more evenly split, with five of 13 expecting a rate increase while eight expect no change to the 2.75 percent overnight rate. \par \par \uc2  In Australia, a survey of 21 economists gave only a 10 percent median chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would move its cash rate from 4.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Economists expect a report on Monday to show U.S. manufacturing expanded at a slower pace in February, with the Institute for Supply Management's index falling to 52.4 from 53.9 in January. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters euro zone purchasing managers index is expected to show contraction in manufacturing slowed slightly, to 49.5 in February from 49.3 in January. \par \par \uc2  Readings above 50 indicate growth in activity while readings below denote contraction. \par \par \uc2  Economic data will likely continue to be overshadowed by developments in Iraq and keep the dollar in a tight range against the euro and yen. \par \par \uc2  "The signals are not clear enough. It is a little too dangerous to trade," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAglobal. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030228dz2s014a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
