\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gains after U.S. durables data, Iraq weighs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2003\par 19:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The dollar rose from a six-month low against the yen and pared losses against the euro on Thursday as unexpectedly strong U.S. durable goods data outweighed concerns over possible war with Iraq for now. \par \par \uc2  Markets have traded with trepidation ahead of an impending debate at the U.N. Security Council, which will deliberate on a new United States and British resolution that could lay the foundation for a military strike against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  One of the concerns weighing most heavily on the dollar, the halting U.S. economy, was temporarily dispelled early in the session when data for January durable goods orders showed a 3.3 percent surge, well above market expectations. \par \par \uc2  With investors still unsettled by a possible armed conflict with Iraq, analysts greeted the figure with measured optimism. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult to envision a real pickup in capital spending until the Iraq concerns are out of the way and profits start to improve, and that's dominating the corporate and economic landscape right now," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal, a research firm in New York. \par \par \uc2  "It is a marginal support for the dollar but it is still playing second fiddle to Iraq concerns. The market is giving priority to news flow on the potential war and possibly on North Korea concerns going ahead," he added. \par \par \uc2  After falling to its weakest level since Sept. 2002 in the London session, the dollar firmed to trade near 117.55 in afternoon U.S. trading, up 0.40 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Though physical evidence was scant, traders strongly suspected that the Bank of Japan discreetly sold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the offshore session at levels near 116.85, helping to support the dollar and dampen the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  "This definitely put a floor under dollar weakness. I would venture to say at this point the market is looking to sell the dollar, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and good data is producing a short squeeze for the dollar overall," said Joe Francomano, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Erste Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  With Wall Street stock indexes notching gains of more than 1 percent each, the euro fell against the dollar to $1.0760, not far from a session low at $1.0737. Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded near 126.50 yen, up 0.20 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains were tempered slightly by a Labor Department report that showed first time jobless claims rose for the second straight week, and that new home sales tumbled 15.1 percent in January. \par \par \uc2  YEN TALK RIFE \par \par \uc2  In Japan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on talk it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a sharp rise in the euro on the yen and the dollar during the London session. \par \par \uc2  Speculation has been rife over the last few days that Japanese authorities have been stepping into the market to sell yen in order to prevent it from causing further pain to the stagnant economy. Such talk has been fueled by confirmation this month by the ministry that it had surreptitiously bought dollars last month. \par \par \uc2  Investors are now looking to Friday's release of of the BoJ's monthly report that may reveal whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month. \par \par \uc2  Michael McGuinness, head of North American sales at American Express Bank in New York, cited market sources who estimated the BoJ purchased $1.5 billion at levels below 117 yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  WEAPON WORRIES \par \par \uc2  A divided U.N. Security Council holds informal talks on Thursday on the U.S.-UK resolution on Iraq as veto-wielding China and Russia argue weapons inspectors should be given more time to search for the country's alleged arsenal of banned arms. \par \par \uc2  Chief arms inspector Hans Blix said he saw Iraq no closer to disarming. He said that while Iraq was offering some new information it still was not fully cooperating with efforts to rid it of banned weapons. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush pushed his case for war, saying on Thursday that any efforts by Iraqi President Saddam Hussein to destroy disputed arms would be "part of a campaign of deception." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030227dz2r00zhu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
