\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF declines comment on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk. \b0\par\par\uc2 230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2003\par 13:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) declined to comment on talk it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after a sharp rise in the euro against the yen and the dollar around 1100 GMT on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Speculation has been rife over the last few days that Japanese authorities have been stepping into the market to contain the yen's recent strength. Such talk has been fuelled by confirmation earlier this month by the ministry that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  discreetly last month. \par \par \uc2  But a MOF official close to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on the latest market movement. \par \par \uc2  "We do not comment on that topic," a MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  The euro spiked up above 126.60 yen  from around 126.20 yen within minutes around 1100 GMT, sparking speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the euro also rose sharply against the dollar, and the dollar/yen exchange rate was steady at that time. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities have made no secret of their displeasure over a sharp rise in the yen which could derail the country's export-led economic recovery. The yen rose to six-month highs against the dollar earlier on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, Hiroshi Watanabe, the head of the MOF's International Bureau, told reporters that Japan was ready to take all necessary measures on exchange rates. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030227dz2