\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar bobs around 6-month low on yen, Iraq weighs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 797 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2003\par 12:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The dollar stumbled to its lowest level in nearly six months versus the yen on Thursday, battered by fears of a U.S.-led war against Iraq and investor attempts to test Japan's resolve to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Markets were increasingly immune to attempts by Japanese officials to talk down the export-hurting yen, arguing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent tactics of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars were not convincing enough to keep flows into yen at bay. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, war jitters also weighed on the dollar, weakening it against the euro and Swiss franc, as the United Nations Security Council geared up for debate on a new U.S.-British resolution that lays the groundwork for armed attack on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen looks extremely vulnerable to weakness," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Financial Corporation in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's partly become a game of cat and mouse. There are no natural yen sellers out there. When you take speculators out of the equation, the only sellers are the authorities," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bobbed around near six-month lows of 116.86 yen in European hours. It slid a third of a percent to $1.0824 per euro and 1.3506 Swiss francs, but was holding well within recent ranges. \par \par \uc2  The euro broke through technical levels against the yen, climbing a quarter percent on the day to 126.55 yen in a move which traders said also helped it higher on the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi said in European trading hours that Japan would take appropriate action against rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, echoing similar comments by Japan's top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi earlier in the day. But he failed to move the yen. \par \par \uc2  Investors are now looking to Friday's release of data that would reveal whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, following market talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been active to stem the yen earlier this week around 117.00-117.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  "There is support at around 116.80 to 116.25 yen, and I think the market is anxious to know whether the authorities will try to defend it or not," said Gunner. \par \par \uc2  WEAPON WORRIES \par \par \uc2  A divided U.N. Security Council holds informal talks on Thursday on the U.S.-UK resolution as veto-wielding China and Russia argue weapons inspectors should be given more time to comb Iraq in search of its alleged arsenal of banned arms. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, chief U.N. weapons inspector Hans Blix said he saw Iraq no closer to disarming. Blix said that while Iraq was offering some new information it still was not fully cooperating with efforts to rid it of banned weapons. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush also pushed the case for war on Wednesday in a nationally-televised speech, saying that the departure of Iraqi President Saddam Hussein would help spread democracy across the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  Sterling fell at the European open after Washington's staunchest ally, British Prime Minister Tony Blair, suffered a major revolt on Wednesday in his own party. \par \par \uc2  "It is essential that Blair secures a second U.N. resolution, or it could be extremely damaging for Blair and political risk premium will build up in sterling," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. \par \par \uc2  But the UK \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  took in its stride a testimony on the euro by Britain's Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown, who refused to be drawn into whether the economic tests for euro entry could be assessed again before the next election if they were not met by the June deadline. \par \par \uc2  Brown also reiterated his long-standing stance that, in principle, he would want Britain to join the euro. \par \par \uc2  FLURRY OF DATA \par \par \uc2  The market is eyeing a heavy data slate in the United States later this afternoon, including weekly jobless claims, January durable goods data and new home sales for January. \par \par \uc2  Jobless claims at 1330 GMT are expected to have fallen to 386,000 from 402,000 a week earlier, although dealers said any figure above 400,000 could presage a weak reading in key monthly jobs data due on Friday next week. \par \par \uc2  Orders for durable goods, also at 1330 GMT, are expected to have risen 1.3 percent in January, compared with an unexpected fall of 0.2 percent in December. New home sales are expected to have slowed to 1,043,000 in January from 1,082,000 in December. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan testifies on the impact of an ageing world population on the U.S. economy at 1500 GMT, while European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Wim Duisenberg speaks in Germany at 1800 GMT. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030227dz2r00nb0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
