\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Oil hits post-Gulf War peak, Euro stocks mixed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nigel Stephenson \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2003\par 10:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 27 (Reuters) - Crude oil prices shot up on Thursday to their highest point since the last Gulf War while fears of war against Iraq saw the dollar fall to its lowest level in almost six months against the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  European stocks eked out small opening gains after the soaring oil price and jitters over Iraq took its toll on Wall Street on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.N. Security Council was to hold informal talks on Thursday on a resolution authorising force against Iraq, with China and Russia saying inspectors should be given more time. \par \par \uc2  Oil, which has been supported for weeks by fears a war would disrupt supplies, charged to new highs as abnormally cold weather in the United States drained heating fuel stocks to their lowest in almost three years. \par \par \uc2  U.S. light crude hit a high of $38.66 a barrel, up 96 cents from New York on Wednesday, before pulling back to $38.27. \par \par \uc2  "There remains a war premium of $2 to $6 on any given day, but there is tightness in fundamentals at the moment and oil prices would certainly be higher than normal, even without Iraq in the picture," said Paul Ashby, an ABN AMRO analyst in Sydney. \par \par \uc2  London Brent crude for April was 50 cents higher at $33.57 a barrel. \par \par \uc2  European shares opened mixed, subdued by the weaker finish on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  The FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips was up 0.36 percent, as was the narrower DJ Euro STOXX 50 index. \par \par \uc2  Investors were looking at results from bellwethers such as Swiss food group Nestle and French insurer AXA. \par \par \uc2  "It's all eyes on the figures this morning. The market's got to digest them and try to get some direction from the street, which is looking a bit directionless at the moment," said a London-based European share trader. \par \par \uc2  SURGING OIL HELPS DEPRESS U.S. STOCKS The surging oil price helped depress U.S. stocks. The Dow Jones Industrial average closed 1.3 percent lower while the tech-laden Nasdaq index ended down 1.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  Lacklustre sales data from Hewlett-Packard, the personal computer and printer maker, drove its shares down 15 percent and added to the gloomy sentiment. \par \par \uc2  But U.S. stock index futures were higher in early European trade, indicating a positive start on Wall Street later in the day. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the Nikkei average wiped out early losses to end 0.03 percent higher to 8,359.38, a shade above a 20-year closing low of 8,303.39 hit last November, as battered banks rebounded and offset pressure on exporters from global tensions and the higher oil prices. \par \par \uc2  The recent strengthening of the yen also kept Tokyo stocks under pressure. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar fell to its lowest in nearly six months versus Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as fears of war outweighed threats of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  "There has been a lot market speculation over whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that is keeping the market very closely focused on dollar/yen," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly fell to 116.90 in Asia, compared with 117.05 in late New York trade on Wednesday. It was last trading at 116.96. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also little changed from the U.S. close at $1.0774, in the middle of recent ranges. \par \par \uc2  Safe-haven euro zone government bond yields, which move inversely to prices, were mixed, reflecting stock market action. \par \par \uc2  The two-year German Schatz note was yielding 2.297 percent, up 1.6 basis points. The 10-year benchmark German Bund was yielding 3.87 percent, down 1.1 basis points and close to a three and a half year low. \par \par \uc2  The market was also looking forward to next week's European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting, which is expected to cut interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "The expectations of a (near-term) rate cut and geopolitics will extend the flight to quality in the short term," one Rome-based debt trader said. Gold was trading at $354.60 an ounce, compared with $353.50 in late New York trade. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030227dz2r00izq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
