\par\par \b \uc2  Yen retreats on Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 933 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2003\par 08:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The yen retreated against the dollar in Asia on Wednesday, dragged down by growing expectations that Japanese authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy more dollars to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moribund export-led economy. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said recent movements in the market had been rapid and inappropriate, and the ministry was watching the market closely. \par \par \uc2  Separately, a senior ministry official said the Group of Seven (G7) nations had agreed at a weekend meeting to cooperate if there were rapid fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which included concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The comments came a day after Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said that G7 ministers had agreed that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be determined by the market, which sent the dollar down to a near six-month low of 117.00 yen in New York. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials worry that a strong yen will hurt exports, the main pillar of the nation's faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said the Bank of Japan (BOJ), which acts as the Finance Ministry's agent, was detected stepping in to buy dollars at around 117.00-117.20 yen in late Tuesday Asian trade and during post-Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi would not comment on the reports, and the market is eagerly awaiting to see BOJ data due out on Friday to confirm whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar, however, struggled to extend gains against the yen as investors remained bearish about the greenback's outlook amid Iraq war jitters and weak U.S. economic data. \par \par \uc2  "Talk was rife after one Japanese bank bid so aggressively for dollars, but we don't want to push the dollar up so much because of uncertainty about the war and the U.S. economy," said a foreign bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow acknowledged during an appearance with U.S. Commerce Secretary Don Evans on CNBC on Tuesday (Wednesday Asian time) that anxiety over the prospects of war has placed a "cloud" over the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "It's restraining spending. It's creating uncertainty among business people....It's taken energy prices higher," Snow said of Americans' concerns about a possible war. \par \par \uc2  "The resolution of the geopolitical situation is the single most important thing to give the economy the lift it needs," he said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0642 GMT, the dollar was at 117.48/51 yen up from 117.25/33 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The euro barely moved at $1.0756/57 staying near its late New York level of $1.0757/61 throughout Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  It was also little changed at 126.33/40 yen \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 , SHIOKAWA \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's comment raised speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  "Although Shiokawa suggested yesterday that (Japan) was unlikely to sell Japanese yen in the near future..., we also think that (Japan) could have sold the yen last night, looking at price actions," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley Japan. \par \par \uc2  Others also saw the remark as a blip, \par \par \uc2  "I think that was just an irregular comment from Shiokawa. I think Japan would forcefully stop the yen's rise if the dollar threatened to break 115 yen," said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late January. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  admitted, long after the fact, that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in January and bought about $6 billion worth of dollars for yen when the greenback fell to around 117.50. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the United States allows \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar is below 120 yen. So I think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Hidenao Miyajima, a strategist at Commerz Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Their secret \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has often taken place in the last week of the month, which is another reason why this week is likely to see Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Miyajima added. \par \par \uc2  BLEAK U.S. SENTIMENT \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise was also capped by an unexpected fall in U.S. consumer confidence as chances of a U.S. attack on Iraq as well as spiking energy prices took a toll on consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Fear of war simmered, with U.S. President George W. Bush on Tuesday emphasising his intention to bypass the United Nations and attack Iraq if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Conference Board released data on Tuesday showing U.S. consumer confidence at its lowest level in nearly 10 years, with the Consumer Confidence Index falling to 64.0 in February from a downwardly revised 78.8 in January. \par \par \uc2  The reading of 64.0 was nearly 15 points lower than January's levels and well below economists' expectations of 76.8 and it was the third consecutive monthly decline. \par \par \uc2  The bleak U.S. sentiment report was in sharp contrast to a better-than-expected reading on Germany's business climate, as measured by the closely watched Ifo index. \par \par \uc2  The index rose to 88.9 in February from 87.4 in January, way ahead of expectations and its highest level in seven months. The Munich-based Ifo reported an improvement in both current conditions and expectations. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, sterling was little changed from late U.S. levels at $1.5743/48 \par \par \uc2  Sterling was pressured across the board after the Bank of England's top economist said Britain's current account deficit may require a further drop in the pound. \par \par \uc2  Bank of England Governor Sir Edward George also said on Tuesday it was hard to know how much further the pound might sink. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030226dz2q00eid\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
