\par\par \b \uc2  Yen retreats as Japan officials repeat warning. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2003\par 04:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The yen retreated a bit against the dollar in Asia on Wednesday morning after Japanese officials said its recent rise was inappropriate and that they were ready to act in the market if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said late in the morning \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had conducted secret \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, buying dollars for yen at around 117 yen. The rate showed no reaction to the disclosure. \par \par \uc2  Despite the move, the dollar had fallen to a near six-month low of 117.00 yen in New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Its dollar's fall was triggered by comments by Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa that G7 ministers meeting last weekend had agreed that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be determined by the market. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's comments raised speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, despite worries by officials that a strong yen hurts export companies. \par \par \uc2  "I think that was just a irregular comment from Shiokawa. I think Japan would forcefully stop the yen's rise if the dollar threatened to break 115 yen," said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Many people think the dollar could easily fall by five to 10 yen once that level is broken. I think it is impossible for Japan to allow that to happen," Ueno said. \par \par \uc2  Finance Ministry officials were busy Wednesday morning trying to extinguish such speculation. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's top financial diplomat, said recent movements in the market had been rapid and inappropriate, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was watching the market closely. \par \par \uc2  G7 AGREEMENT \par \par \uc2  Separately, a senior ministry official said the G7 agreed to cooperate if there were rapid fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which included concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The official, asked if Japanese authorities had secretly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, said authorities would act as appropriate while watching market conditions closely. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's apparent move followed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late January. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  admitted, long after the fact, that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in January and bought about $6 billion worth of dollars for yen when the dollar fell to around 117.50. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the Unites States allows \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar is below 120 yen. So I think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Hidenao Miyajima, a strategist at Commerz Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Their secret \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has often taken place in the last week of the month, which is another reason why this week is likely to see Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Miyajima added. \par \par \uc2  As of 0246 GMT, the dollar was at 117.45/57 up from 117.25/33 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $1.0748/53 staying near its late New York level of $1.0757/61 throughout Asian morning trade. \par \par \uc2  It was also little changed at 126.18/29 yen \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also hurt by an unexpected fall in U.S. consumer confidence as chances of a U.S. attack on Iraq as well as spiking energy prices took a toll on consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Fear of war simmered, with U.S. President George W. Bush on Tuesday emphasising his intention to bypass the United Nations and attack Iraq if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Conference Board released data on Tuesday showing U.S. consumer confidence at its lowest level in nearly 10 years, with the Consumer Confidence Index falling to 64.0 in February from a downwardly revised 78.8 in January. \par \par \uc2  BLEAK U.S. SENTIMENT \par \par \uc2  The reading of 64.0 was nearly 15 points lower than January's levels and well below economists' expectations of 76.8 and it was the third consecutive monthly decline. \par \par \uc2  The bleak U.S. sentiment report was in sharp contrast to a better-than-expected reading on Germany's business climate, as measured by the closely watched Ifo index. \par \par \uc2  The index rose to 88.9 in February from 87.4 in January, way ahead of expectations and its highest level in seven months. The Munich-based Ifo reported an improvement in both current conditions and expectations. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, sterling was little changed from late U.S. levels at $1.5743/48 \par \par \uc2  Sterling was pressured across the board after the Bank of England's top economist said Britain's current account deficit may require a further drop in the pound. \par \par \uc2  Bank of England Governor Sir Edward George also said on Tuesday it was hard to know how much further the pound might sink. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030226dz2q005pm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
