\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Slips Against Yen to The Dismay of Tokyo \b0\par\par\uc2 961 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2003\par 00:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -- The dollar fell moderately against its major  rivals\par Wednesday in New York, reversing gains posted against the yen earlier in\par the global session when talk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helped buoy the greenback.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  War rhetoric and a sagging stock market amid uncertainty  about the world economy and U.S. plans for military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Middle East ultimately undermined the dollar's gains, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar was at &#165;117.13, well off the intraday high of &#165;117.60, but down slightly from &#165;117.25 posted in late New York trading Tuesday. The euro was at $1.0778, up from its intraday low of $1.0733 and from $1.0763 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Growing speculation that the Japanese government launched a covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Tuesday gained momentum in Tokyo and London trading Wednesday after a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official attempted to clarify comments by Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa earlier this week that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not be likely. "[Shiokawa] didn't mean \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left entirely up to the market," the official said. \par \par \uc2  Early in New York trading, traders noted activity that suggested some major financial institutions might be acting on behalf of Japanese authorities, but given the nature of these covert \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , no one can be sure until hard numbers, such as monthly reserve data, are made public. \par \par \uc2  "It's not the kind of thing where we get the kind of signs we use to get," said Citibank chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Sinche. \par \par \uc2  While some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiated its first covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month in an effort to prevent market participants from targeting specific areas of  support, others say the reason may be more politically driven. \par \par \uc2  "An important motive to Japan's secrecy lies in the perception that Japan is protecting its own export-driven recovery at the expense of its trading partners," said MG Financial chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Ashraf Laidi in a research note. U.S. manufacturers have repeatedly complained that a strong dollar is hurting business at home. \par \par \uc2  Whatever the reason, the MOF is facing an uphill battle if it is indeed manipulating the value of the yen, say strategists. The Japanese fiscal year end on March 31 and the nation's current account deficit conspire to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strong. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3561 francs, down slightly from 1.3566 francs late Tuesday in New  York. Sterling was at $1.5811, compared with $1.5745 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Equities didn't provide the greenback any support, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average falling 1.3%. \par \par \uc2  In the end, a number of news items including hawkish comments from U.N. chief weapons inspector Hans Blix and U.S. Deputy Defense Secretary Paul Wolfowitz as well as a change in the composition of Russia's foreign reserves forced the dollar lower. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at the U.N., Mr. Blix said that while Iraq has clarified some outstanding issues surrounding its weapons program, Saddam Hussien has not indicated that he is planning to disarm. Mr. Blix said the Iraqi leader has handed over a number of letters regarding its arsenal of weapons, but the information does not represent "full cooperation or a breakthrough." The destruction of the Al Samoud 2 missiles by Saturday is of greater importance, said  Mr. Blix, who reiterated that the issue is not negotiable. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Mr. Wolfowitz repeated the Bush Administration mantra that Iraq is "not cooperating" with U.N. weapons inspectors, adding that he hoped U.N. Security Council members realized that the credibility of the U.N. is  on the line. The U.S. official also said the U.S. has "a problem with" North Korea, which reminded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders that a shift in U.S. foreign policy away from containment to  preventive measures will keep geopolitical events in the forefront of factors driving the direction of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  News that Russia plans to move more of its reserves into  the euro at the expense of the greenback fueled declines in  the dollar against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Russia's candid admission Wednesday that it will cut the dollar portion of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to 50% from 70% and raise the euro slice to 30% from 25%, is feeding a growing sense in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that such diversification by central banks is only set to gather steam. \par \par \uc2  The euro got an immediate lift during New York trading from First Deputy Finance Minister Alexei Ulyukayev's statement, indicating that even though Russia still considers it "important" to hold a fairly significant amount  of dollar reserves, it will substantially lower its own holdings. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Ulyukayev said that 10% of Russia's reserves should be in gold and the rest in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Swiss franc and sterling. Currently, gold and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make up 5% of reserves. \par \par \uc2  While not hugely significant in and of itself -- Russia's roughly $47.8 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings are just one tenth the size of Japan's -- the unusually frank statement of intent from the Russian authorities has fueled questions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as to whether other  banks might start to follow suit. \par \par \uc2  "This has given additional life to the view that one of the supporting facts for the euro going forward will be reserve diversification," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign  exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  Write to Agnes T. Crane at agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -- Grainne McCarthy and John Parry contributed to this report \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  02-26-03 1850ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030226dz2q009ex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
