\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options:Dlr/Yen Vols Lower As Dlr Sits Above Y117.0 \b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2003\par 22:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were lower Wednesday afternoon in New York compared with levels earlier Wednesday in Tokyo, while one-month euro/dollar options volatilities were slightly higher. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen volatilities were lower because of continuing fears about the possibility of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which were putting a floor under the dollar just above the key Y117.0 mark. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities were reacting to the perception that against the yen, the dollar "is just sitting there," with its descent checked by "rumblings there's a big bid on Y117.0 and below," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  options salesperson at a bank based in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar retreated moderately against the yen as well as its other major rivals, hurt by a decline on U.S. equity markets, persistent worries about the drumbeats of a potential U.S.-led war with Iraq and also by a Russian finance ministry official's candid admission Wednesday that Russia will cut the dollar portion of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to 50% from 70%. \par \par \uc2  Around 3:00 p.m. EST (2000 GMT) in New York, the euro was at $1.0781, up from $1.0763 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at Y117.04, down from Y117.25 in late New York dealings Tuesday. The dollar was also at CHF1.3553, down from CHF1.3566 and sterling was at $1.5797, up from $1.5745 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 9.8%-10.1%, down from 10.05%-10.4% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar option volatilities were at 9.5%-9.9%, modestly up from 9.4%-9.7% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favored dollar puts at 0.7%-1.1%, compared with 0.9%-1.05% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls were favored over the dollar at 0.4%-0.8% in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry, Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com;\par 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030226dz2q001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
