\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - BoJ move means weak yen uncertain, not impossible. \b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2003\par 00:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In NEW YORK story headlined "BoJ move means weak yen\par uncertain, not impossible" please read in 3rd paragraph ....would\par succeed Masaru Hayami.... instead of ....would succeed Haruhiko\par Kuroda (correcting name).\par Also, please read in 14th paragraph....former Vice Finance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Minister Toshiro Muto (adding dropped word "former").\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  A corrected story follows. \par \par \uc2  By Javier David \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 25 (Reuters) - Some market participants are wondering, with the appointment of a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor who hews to traditional monetary views, whether the irresistible force of yen policy may be running headlong into an immovable object. \par \par \uc2  Not necessarily, say analysts. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yen surged on news that Toshihiko Fukui, a policy conservative and career bureaucrat, would succeed Masaru Hayami as Bank of Japan governor. \par \par \uc2  Finance officials have tried systematically to guide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower by selling yen in favor of dollars. \par \par \uc2  But that stance was thrown into doubt on Tuesday when Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said he agreed with his Group of Seven counterparts in Paris last weekend that exchange rates should be determined by markets, although he did not rule out countering extreme or speculative movements. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economy suffers from multiple ailments. One is a relentless fall in prices, a condition many economists say could be cured by the government adopting a target for inflation and purchasing foreign bonds, both of which would facilitate a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  Fukui has resisted such measures in the past, and although he is not as implacable on the subject as his predecessor, traders rapidly sent the yen to six-month highs against the dollar and one-month peaks against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Market observers, however, see Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remaining squarely in the domain of the Finance Ministry, which most believe wants a weaker yen despite the confusion over Shiokawa's remarks or the changeover at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "In terms of the BoJ change of command, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in Japan, as in the States, is a matter for the finance ministry. I don't think we'll see too much of a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics in Essex, Connecticut. \par \par \uc2  "Fukui is not going to lead (Japan) down a massive foreign bond purchasing path that would translate into regular rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Gilmore said. "A weak yen won't be embraced or incorporated into BoJ policy but could be conducted on the MoF's (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's) orders, and probably with less resistance" than under Hayami, he added. \par \par \uc2  YEN AND YANG \par \par \uc2  Under outgoing BoJ Governor Hayami's tenure, the value of the yen was a constant source of friction between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the finance ministry. \par \par \uc2  Officials need the yen weak to keep Japanese exports cheap in global markets, but Hayami argued against such measures, branding them as worthless without instituting painful structural reforms only politicians could make. \par \par \uc2  Ever bullish on the yen, Hayami on Tuesday issued a withering critique of those who have sought a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the economy, calling such moves "useless" in the face of Japan's strong economic potential. \par \par \uc2  But analysts don't see Fukui as a necessarily dyed-in-the-wool opponent of a weaker yen. Some think the appointment of Kazumasa Iwata, known as a proponent of unorthodox measures, and former Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto as Fukui's deputies could lead to a gradual, longer-term softening in the BoJ's official stance. \par \par \uc2  "People were hoping for someone a bit more aggressive who would choose a slightly more drastic option" for the economy, said Francesca Forasnari, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in New York. But "the one thing that has changed after yesterday's announcement is the heavyweights of the BoJ and MoF are aligning closer." \par \par \uc2  Bernard Connolly, an economist at AIG Trading Group in Greenwich, Connecticut, said the appointments of Muto "cemented" the MoF's control over the bank. But he indicated the BoJ's new regime would hardly become handmaidens for those seeking an extreme change in monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's "rejection for the BoJ job of advocates of inflation targets and BoJ foreign bond purchases reduces the possibility that the process of weakening the yen will be undertaken in the near future," Connolly wrote in a research note to clients on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030225dz2p011nn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
