\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Up Against Rivals on Dismal U.S. Economic Data \b0\par\par\uc2 1,110 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2003\par 00:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -- The dollar bungee-jumped Tuesday, careening lower on some\par dismal U.S. economic data, before whiplashing back on hopes that a war\par with Iraq could yet be averted and ending higher against most major\par rivals.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Traders are on heightened alert for the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar slipped to seven-month lows against the yen around &#165;117, while the pound fell quite sharply, encouraged by Bank of England officials. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday in New York, the dollar was at 117.25 yen, off its global session lows but down quite sharply from 117.90 yen late Monday in New York. The euro was well off its global session highs, at $1.0763 and down from $1.0792 late Monday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3566 Swiss francs, modestly up from 1.3557 francs late Monday. Sterling was at $1.5745, steeply down from $1.5867 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The pound plumbed four-year lows against the euro and for two months against the dollar, after Bank of England governor Eddie George told the U.K.'s Treasury Select Committee Tuesday that the "lowering of sterling and the dollar against the euro is not unhelpful in the context of the international imbalances we face ... it's going in the right kind of direction." \par \par \uc2  Although the harbingers of a potential war are still clouding dollar sentiment, the greenback's resilience against the euro shows that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be embarking on a retreat, said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  President Bush and Defense Secretary Donald Rumsfeld both sounded hawkish about military options Tuesday. Mr. Rumsfeld said troops are ready to launch an attack on Iraq if necessary, while Mr. Bush said Iraq can avoid war only with "full disarmament" and reiterated his view that the U.S. can launch a strike on Iraq with or without a new U.N. resolution. \par \par \uc2  Despite a continuing spat among U.N. members over how to  push Iraqi President Saddam Hussein to disarm weapons of mass destruction, any failure to pass a second U.N. resolution against Mr. Hussein, "will not lead to a change in U.S. war plans," said Mr. Chandler. \par \par \uc2  But that perception failed to taint dollar sentiment much Tuesday, he noted while even some stronger-than-expected euro-zone economic data, contrasted with a dismal U.S. consumer confidence report dented the dollar against the euro, but only temporarily. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer confidence plunged to its lowest level in nearly 10 years in February, the Conference Board reported, as war jitters, rising energy prices and weak labor markets hammered consumer sentiment. The group said its index of consumer confidence for February plunged nearly 15 points to  64.0, from a revised 78.8 in January. \par \par \uc2  "Lackluster job and financial markets, rising fuel costs, and the increasing threat of war and terrorism, appear to have taken a toll on consumers," said Conference Board economist Lynn Franco in a press release. \par \par \uc2  The dollar took a swift leg down in the wake of the widely watched sentiment report, hitting global session lows  against some major rivals after the data raised fresh fears  about the vitality of consumer spending, which comprises two thirds of U.S. economic activity. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell briefly below the 1.3500 franc mark against the Swiss franc, while the euro broke fleetingly above the $1.08 level, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was dented by the data's strong signal that the economy may be weaker than  previously thought. \par \par \uc2  "This is a very troubling number" in terms of its potentially negative longer term effects on foreign demand for equities and dollar-denominated assets, said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  The gloom from the very poor consumer confidence report were soon dispelled however, as remarks by chief U.N. weapons inspector Hans Blix proved one catalyst reviving the  greenback in a swift, decisive rebound. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Blix said Tuesday that letters from Iraq in the past  few days indicate some progress, although he added that Mr.  Hussein still hasn't officially responded to a U.N. demand to destroy its Al Samoud 2 missiles. \par \par \uc2  Those comments appear to have driven the dollar's sharp relief rally, said Larry Brickman, interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign  exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar had slid early in the session, continuing to digest the implications of news of a new Bank of Japan governor announced Monday, and following some mixed signals from Japanese officials Tuesday on their willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  After months of speculation, Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has picked former Bank of Japan Deputy Governor Toshihiko Fukui to replace outgoing BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami, asking the ruling coalition to begin procedures for securing his appointment in the Diet. \par \par \uc2  The selection of Mr. Fukui, a middle-of-the-road candidate who is not likely to take aggressive steps designed to stoke price pressures in Japan's moribund economy, "has caused a huge stir in the yen," said Thomas Sowanick, chief global fixed income strategist with Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2  Analysts consulted for this report said they hadn't seen  any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday undertaken by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which acts on behalf of the ministry in such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, the apparent stability of the dollar in the early part of the New York trading session has raised suspicions that Japanese commercial banks may have been buying dollars against yen, possibly on behalf of the Japanese government, some said. \par \par \uc2  "I see a lot of people selling the dollar against the yen and (the dollar) is not moving down as sharply as I would expect, given all the sales," said Larry Kantor, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at JP Morgan. Because there appears to be considerable downward pressure on the greenback in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market against the yen, the dollar's resilience at certain levels "suggests to me that something is going on," Mr. Kantor said. \par \par \uc2  It is plausible that Japanese commercial banks are simply attempting to front-run any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ, bidding up the dollar against the yen on expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step into the market, Mr. Kantor speculated. \par \par \uc2  --Agnes T. Crane contributed to this report. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  02-25-03