\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: N Korea Adds To Jitters \b0\par\par\uc2 2,783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2003\par 06:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Missile Firing Fuels More Mkt Worry\par DJIA          7858.24    loss 159.80    dn 2.0%\par NASDAQ        1322.38    loss 26.64     dn 2.0%\par S&P 500       832.58     loss 15.59     dn 1.8%\par Dow Future    7813.00    loss 47.00     dn 0.6%\par NASDAQ Future 990.00     loss 4.00      dn 0.4%\par S&P Future    827.90     loss 4.70      dn 0.6%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.84% dn 0.05\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Wall Street posted its biggest decline in a month Monday as the United States and Britain submitted a new U.N. Security Council resolution authorizing war against Iraq. Retail stocks also sagged on forecasts of sluggish sales. The price of safety - Treasurys - rose. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: "It's continuing concerns about Iraq and that Iraq will fail to comply with a U.N. order to destroy missiles," said Alan Skrainka, chief market strategist at Edward Jones of St. Louis. "Retailers are weak given the lack of confidence among consumers and investors." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. and Britain introduced to the Security Council Monday a resolution that declares Iraq has violated its obligations to disarm, while France, Germany and Russia circulated a counterproposal for step-by-step disarmament of Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Saddam Hussein, meanwhile, indicated in an interview with CBS anchor Dan Rather that he did not intend to follow U.N. orders to destroy his Al-Samoud 2 missiles. \par \par \uc2  Retailers fell after several reported that severe winter weather will depress February sales. Wal-Mart lost $1.26 to $47.64, while Federated Department Stores dropped 76 cents to $24.82, and J.C. Penney declined 95 cents to $19.39. \par \par \uc2  Ahold tumbled $6.53, or 61%, to $4.16 after the global grocery store operator said it overstated earnings in 2001 and 2002 by at least $500 million and that its leading executives will resign. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: U.S. Treasurys advanced Monday, helped by the drop in U.S. stock markets and ongoing concern about a war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Long-maturity Treasurys led the charge, as short maturities were hindered by the specter of new supply announced Monday. The Treasury said it will sell $27 billion in new two-year notes Wednesday, and Fannie Mae is slated to sell a $4 billion three-year issue Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Market anxiety ahead of the potential start of military engagement should continue to keep Treasurys fairly well-bid," said Michael Ryan, fixed-income strategist at UBS PaineWebber in New York. \par \par \uc2  A quarterly survey of economists released by the Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia showed they look for growth in the first half of 2003 to be at a slow 2.5% annual pace. But they see it picking up to a 3.5% pace in the second half of the year. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday brings releases on February consumer confidence levels from the Conference Board and January existing home sales from the National Association of Realtors. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Shares Plunge On Missile Test\par USD-Yen             117.48       loss  0.43    dn 0.4%\par AUD-USD             0.6056       gain  0.0008  up 0.2%\par Nikkei 225          8351.78      loss  213.17  dn 2.5%\par Hang Seng           9123.18      loss  116.29  dn 1.2%\par JGB Yield           0.7800% down 0.0250\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks plunged Tuesday morning, as markets\par reacted to the naming of a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief, to news that North\par Korea test-fired a missile into the Sea of Japan and to the lower\par dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo shares responded poorly to the naming of Toshihiko Fukui late\par Monday as the next Bank of Japan chief. Analysts said investors were\par disappointed by Fukui, seen as a conservative choice unlikely to take\par tough action against the deflation crippling the world's second-largest\par economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market's fall was exacerbated by news that North Korea had\par test-fired a missile into the Sea of Japan the previous day. The news\par rattled investors, recalling that North Korea fired a multistage missile\par in August 1989 that flew over Japan. That launch proved that Pyongyang\par is capable of striking any part of Japanese territory.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Major blue-chips were down across the board, including automakers\par Honda and Toyota. Export-dependent technology issues Sony, Toshiba and\par NEC also lost ground.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a stern warning that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets after the yen surged overnight, Finance Minister Masajuro\par Shiokawa said Tuesday that authorities might need to "show an aggressive\par stance."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Shares Seen Adding To Losses\par Euro-USD            1.0788      loss  0.0008  dn 0.1%\par Stlg-USD            1.5870      loss  0.0005  dn 0.04%\par USD-Franc           1.3571      gain  0.0010  up 0.1%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European stocks are expected to fall and\par government bond prices to rise, with the euro set to attack $1.0800.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares are expected to add to Monday's losses after\par North Korea's missile test piled on more uncertainty in the market.\par Plus, a swarm of earnings reports may keep many investors at bay.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks retreated Monday after both the chief executive and\par the chief financial officer at Ahold NV resigned following the emergence\par of accounting irregularities at the Dutch supermarket giant.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors were rattled by memories of the accounting scandals that\par rocked markets during 2002.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market direction is likely to remain jittery as the end game on the\par Iraq crisis plays out, E*Trade Securities analyst Rupert Thompson said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to Merrill Lynch, European equities have continued to\par struggle while credit spreads have narrowed significantly. "Earnings\par power is weak and so are equities. In contrast, credit spreads are\par narrowing as cash flow surges at the corporate sector," analysts said in\par a note published Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds are heading into a higher opening on\par concern over Iraq and North Korea.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Monday, euro-zone government bond rose in price, and the yield\par curve steepened. Comments by European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim\par Duisenberg have boosted hopes of a rate cut in March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The very long end has underperformed other maturities, with the yield\par on the 30-year bund falling by just one basis point.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are some rumors about U.K. pension funds liquidating holdings,"\par a market participant said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This session at 0900 GMT, the German Ifo business climate index is\par expected to have been unchanged in February from 87.4 in January, where\par it rose for the first time since May last year. Analysts are cautious on\par the overall outlook of business sentiment because of the euro's recent\par strengthening and possible war in Iraq. But current conditions should\par have improved from depressed sentiment at end of 2002.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro is hovering close to $1.08 as the market figures\par potential war supports the euro even if interest-rate differentials are\par likely to narrow.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "War jitters are creeping into the game and you are getting some\par slippage on both equities and the dollar," said David Mozina, head of\par G10 strategy with Banc of America Securities in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chief weapons inspector Hans Blix reiterated to reporters at the\par United Nations that the destruction of Iraqi missiles is not open to\par negotiation. Iraq must start destroying the missiles - as well as the\par engines and components for them - by Saturday, as demanded in a letter\par Blix recently sent to Iraqi leaders.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also driving investor jitters, the U.S., Britain and Spain submitted a\par resolution to the U.N. Security Council declaring that Saddam Hussein\par has missed "the final opportunity" to disarm peacefully.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the Australian and New Zealand dollars jumped sharply\par against their U.S. counterpar, after hints of a forthcoming interest\par rate cut in the euro zone fueled the appetite for so-called commodity\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gold, meanwhile, hit $360 an ounce on the mounting concern over\par potential conflict.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Tuesday, Feb 25: German IFO, U.S. Confidence\par ET/GMT\par 0200/0700 Germany  Jan Import Prices\par 0245/0745 France   Fourth-Quarter Housing Starts\par 0245/0745 France   January Consumer Spending\par 0250/0750 France   January Final Consumer Price Index\par 0330/0830 Italy    February Business Confidence Survey\par 0400/0900 Germany  February West German IFO Business\par                    Sentiment Survey (seen 