\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar's Slide Vs Yen Puts Traders On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Alert \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry and Agnes T. Crane \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2003\par 16:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar's slide to 25-week lows against the yen Tuesday has set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders intently second-guessing whether Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be at work behind the scenes, trying to check the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Analysts consulted for this report early Tuesday morning in New York emphasized that they hadn't seen any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertaken by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which acts on behalf of the ministry in such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, the apparent stability of the dollar in the early part of the New York trading session has raised suspicions that Japanese commercial banks may be buying dollars against yen possibly on behalf of the Japanese government, some said. \par \par \uc2  "I see a lot of people selling the dollar against the yen and (the dollar) is not moving down as sharply as I would expect, given all the sales," said Larry Kantor, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at JP Morgan. Because there appears to be more downward pressure on the greenback in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market against the yen, "that suggests to me that something is going on," Kantor said. \par \par \uc2  It is also plausible that Japanese commercial banks are simply attempting to front-run any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ, bidding up the dollar against the yen on expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step into the market, Kantor speculated. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders had already become nervous Monday about the possibility of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of  the levels that the dollar reached against the yen, ending Monday's New York session around Y117.90. These levels are comparable to those where the BOJ was thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly during January. \par \par \uc2  Since the BOJ broke with tradition and launched \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by stealth last month, announcing its actions days after the event, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants have become more jittery about the possibility that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand may be invisibly at work. \par \par \uc2  "It's not clear whether they've done anything or not" given the strength of the yen in recent trading sessions, but they are "not going to announce it," said Robert Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in New York. He reckons the BOJ will enter the market during Tokyo time on Wednesday to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under wraps. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during New York trading hours, it would have to do so through third parties in the U.S., increasing the chances of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  being spotted, says Sinche. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I'm surprised that there hasn't been more activity out" of the\par finance ministry, given the strong comments from Finance Minister\par Masajuro Shiokawa overnight, Sinche said. Shiokawa said authorities\par might need to "show an aggressive stance."\par    In a mixed message however, Shiokawa also said that   markets ought\par to be left to their own devices to determine exchange rates, signaling\par he has no plans to strong arm the yen sharply lower against other major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Outgoing Bank of Japan governor Masaru Hayami also blasted\par the finance ministry for its efforts to weaken the yen. These comments\par only helped the yen extend its rally.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen's latest surge was sparked Monday, when after months of\par speculation, Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi selected former\par Bank of Japan Deputy Governor Toshihiko Fukui to replace outgoing BOJ\par Governor Masaru Hayami. Koizumi has asked the ruling coalition to begin\par procedures for securing his appointment in the Diet.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The choice of a policy conservative who will likely perpetuate the\par status quo was a disappointment for those buying into Koizumi's\par oft-repeated mantra that he wanted a dedicated deflation fighter from\par the private sector at the helm of the BOJ. The selection makes hefty\par quantitative easing measures that would weaken the yen less likely and\par has helped the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  firm.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A firmer yen is the last thing the world's second biggest economy\par needs, because it could snuff out the tentative signs of recovery in\par Japan's export sector, while economic activity as a whole looks set to\par relapse.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Having sagged to around Y117.0 during the European session trading\par session, the dollar has being flitting not far above the Y117.0 mark\par during the early part of the New York morning, further pressured by a\par plunge in consumer confidence to its lowest point in almost 10 years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around 9:45 a.m. EST, the dollar was trading at Y117.10, down from\par Y117.19 early Tuesday morning and quite steeply down from Y117.90 late\par Monday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.0807, up from $1.0791\par earlier in London and  from $1.0792 late Monday. Against the Swiss\par franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3512, down from CHF1.3557 late Monday.\par Sterling was at $1.5755, down from $1.5867.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry and Agnes T. Crane; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par    -By Agnes T. Crane; Dow Jones Newswires\par    agnes.crane@dowjones.com; 201-938-2248\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030225dz2p005pm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
