\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen rallies as Japan appoints new central banker. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2003\par 19:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 24 (Reuters) - The yen surged to one-month peaks against the dollar and euro on Monday, as Japan's nomination of a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head led to speculation no new policies would be adopted that will result in a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  Toshihiko Fukui - a career bureaucrat and strong proponent of traditional monetary policy - was nominated to replace Masaru Hayami at the helm of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  The news came on the heels of a weekend meeting in Paris of the finance officials of the world's seven largest industrialized nations. \par \par \uc2  Markets had hoped for a candidate who might take a more aggressive approach to the country's problems. \par \par \uc2  "The interpretation is that people had been expecting something dramatic out of the Group of Seven meeting that would connote yen weakness, or they were expecting ... someone else at BOJ that would pursue yen weakness," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at ABN AMRO Bank in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  Fukui's appointment was seen as positive for the yen in light of his perceived reluctance to take unorthodox monetary easing measures, such as inflation targeting and the purchase of foreign assets. Economists say such steps could devalue the yen but energize Japan's moribund economy. \par \par \uc2  But Anderson called the current rally "dangerous," given that Japanese authorities are actively seeking a weaker yen to counteract the effects of a slowing economy. "We're right down at the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels of the past," he said, which raises risks \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step into the market to sell yen. \par \par \uc2  Renewed fears about a potential war with Iraq pulled stocks lower and the dollar followed suit, falling against the euro to $1.08 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar bought 117.70 yen  at the U.S. midday, its lowest level\par since Jan. 27 and down 0.85 percent from its previous close. The euro\par traded near 126.40 yen          , down 0.50 percent on the day and just\par above its lowest level since Jan. 22.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar surged to a two-year high against the dollar  to\par C$1.49, supported by increased investor purchases of Canadian\par fixed-income assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One IMM floor trader noted a German bank was buying euro futures in\par bulk on the open, while another said traders bought the yen contract\par aggressively in overnight electronic action, partly on bullish\par sentiment generate by Fukui's appointment to the BOJ.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NO IMPETUS FROM G7, MARKET EYES IRAQ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G7 meeting generally produced little by way of surprising remarks\par about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements, which analysts interpreted to mean the group\par was not bothered by current trends in the market. In its communique,\par the group issued the standard refrain on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that it would\par "continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as\par necessary."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, news regarding a potential war on Iraq continued to weigh on\par the dollar. On Monday, U.N. Security Council diplomats said a new\par U.S.-British resolution that would be introduced would set the stage\par for a war to disarm Iraqi President Saddam Hussein. The resolution\par would declare Baghdad in violation of U.N demands over weapons of mass\par destruction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Certainly geopolitical sentiments are having an impact day to day and\par hour to hour," said Michael Woolfolk, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Bank of New\par York. "I fully expect that we're going to be in this position\par responding to issues pertaining to Iraq ... for some time."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EURO RATE CUT EYED\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In offshore trading, the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened after European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg suggested at the weekend that the\par bank may cut interest rates. Duisenberg said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's hands\par would not be tied by a possible Iraq war, which was interpreted as a\par clear signal that the ECB could cut interest rates in the near term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ECB's key rate is currently 2.75 percent and will next be reviewed\par at the bank's policy meeting on March 6. Because higher interest rates\par in the euro zone have been one of the main attractions of the euro, a\par rate cut could damage the euro's outlook.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 1741GMT. All data taken from Reuters\par calculated from the levels at 5:30 p.m. (22:30 GMT) in the\par previous New York session.\par                       Last  US Close    %      YTD     2002\par                              21 Feb.  Change %Change    Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par Euro/dlr             1.0798   1.0765   +0.31   +2.88   1.0496\par Dlr/yen              117.72   118.69   -0.82   -0.86   118.74\par Euro/yen             127.14   127.71   -0.45   +1.98   124.67\par Dlr/swiss            1.3569   1.3652   -0.61   -1.76   1.3812\par Stg/dlr              1.5868   1.5792   +0.48   -1.50   1.6110\par Dlr/cad              1.4909   1.5044   -0.90   -5.17   1.5722\par Aus/dlr              0.6061   0.5969   +1.54   +8.06   0.5609\par Euro/swiss           1.4655   1.4689   -0.23   +1.10   1.4496\par Euro/stg             0.6801   0.6811   -0.15   +4.44   0.6512\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par  Economic Forecasts...          Official rates...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......             Top events........\par  Diaries...........        Diaries Index........\par  Press Digests....         Polls on G7 economies..\par  European markets......\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030224dz2o01175\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
