\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Declines Against Rivals on BOJ Choice, War Worries \b0\par\par\uc2 979 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2003\par 00:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -- The dollar suffered a double whammy Monday, falling first\par against the yen after a conservative policy maker was nominated as the\par next Bank of Japan governor, then  punished more broadly as harbingers\par of a potential war with  Iraq appeared to intensify.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The dollar also fell quite sharply against the so-called  "commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", including the Australian and New Zealand dollars, while the Canadian dollar hit its strongest  level in two years. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday afternoon in New York, the dollar was trading at 117.90 yen, off its global session lows, but still down almost a full yen from 118.75 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.0792, back below the $1.08 mark it had broken briefly above, but up from $1.0761 late Friday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.3557 francs, quite steeply down from 1.3652 francs late Friday. Sterling was at $1.5867, up from $1.5796 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "War jitters are creeping into the game and you are getting some slippage on both equities and the dollar," said  David Mozina, head of G10 strategy with Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  Chief weapons inspector Hans Blix reiterated to reporters at the U.N. that the destruction of Iraqi missiles  isn't open to negotiation. Iraq must start destroying the missiles -- as well as the engines and components for them -- by Saturday, as demanded in a letter Mr. Blix recently sent to Iraqi leaders. \par \par \uc2  Also driving investor jitters, the U.S., Britain and Spain submitted a resolution to the U.N. Security Council declaring that Saddam Hussein has missed "the final opportunity" to disarm peacefully. \par \par \uc2  While the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 1.9%, the dollar slipped below the 1.3600 mark against the Swiss franc, a classic refuge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in times of war. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also depressed by the decidedly unadventurous choice for the new BOJ governor, which dispelled illusions Japan will make more aggressive efforts to fight deflation. \par \par \uc2  After months of speculation, Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi selected former BOJ Deputy Governor Toshihiko Fukui to replace outgoing BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami, asking the ruling coalition to begin procedures for securing his appointment in the Diet. \par \par \uc2  The choice was a disappointment for those buying into Mr. Koizumi's oft-repeated mantra that he wanted a dedicated  deflation fighter from the private sector at the helm of the BOJ. In the end, he chose an establishment candidate who  is little different from the incumbent, only firming a sense of cynicism surrounding any reform efforts in the world's second largest economy. \par \par \uc2  "It's a classic Japanese compromise really," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Mellon Bank in London. "It's the same old stuff," where Japan's policy makers aren't likely to take drastic measures in an effort to wrest the economy out of its long running deflationary spiral, he added. \par \par \uc2  Now, the yen's strength, which threatens to snuff out any prospects of an export-led recovery, raises the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepping in to undertake \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The MOF acts via the BOJ in such moves. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's revelation that it undertook covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen last month already has traders jumpy at the prospect of a repeat performance, especially as the dollar has fallen below the key &#165;118.0 on Monday. The greenback is already at roughly the same levels where the BOJ was thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could occur "at any moment," warned Robert Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Citibank in New York.  The question on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders minds' is whether the &#165;117.0 area would offer any support for the dollar, or whether it will fall through that over the next two global trading sessions, Mr. Sinche said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Australian and New Zealand dollars jumped  sharply against their U.S. counterpart Monday, after hints of a forthcoming interest rate cut in the euro zone fueled the appetite for so-called commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Two weeks after saying that any rate cut now would "drown in the sea of uncertainties," European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said over the weekend that the ECB "will not hesitate to act" if its assessment of the outlook for inflation changes. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders immediately viewed the comment, made during the Paris meeting of policy makers from the Group of Seven nations, as a clear signal that the ECB could ease policy as early as its next meeting, scheduled for Thursday,  March 6. \par \par \uc2  For commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this is stoking interest as an  ECB cut will widen the already substantial interest-rate differential between the euro and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar, rendering it even more attractive for investors to buy higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are so called because of the dependence of their economies on commodities. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar also extended its impressive 2003 rally, surging to its best levels in over two years as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors continued to be attracted by Canada's robust economy, although prospects for additional near-term appreciation may be more limited, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar slipped through support around $1.5000 Canadian, falling as far as the $1.4900 Canadian figure for the first time since early February 2001, perpetuating a rally that began in the first days of this year when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovering around $1.5800 Canadian. \par \par \uc2  -- Paul Evans in Toronto contributed to this report \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com and Agnes  T. Crane at agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  02-24-03 1843ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030224dz2o005v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
