\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr/Yen Dn; Disappointing Signs On BOJ Chief \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2003\par 06:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar slid more than half a yen in Asia Monday, as traders were disappointed by reports Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi may choose a traditionalist to run the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Lingering hopes for an aggressive deflation fighter at the helm of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were battered as local media reported the government is poised to appoint the conservative former BOJ deputy governor Toshihiko Fukui as the next governor. \par \par \uc2  Speculators and interbank players quickly dumped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the reports. BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami steps down on March 19. \par \par \uc2  "Fukui is the establishment's candidate and would continue with Hayami's line," said Marshall Gittler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The reports from the Yomiuri Shimbun and other media gave market players good reason to doubt the BOJ would implement radical monetary easing such as inflation targeting, which would result in a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's plunge was also fueled by healthy sales of euros for yen by Japanese investors and overseas players taking profits, amid notions that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may ease monetary policy soon. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese insurance firms took profits on euro-denominated securities holdings while overseas speculators took profits on long euro positions against the yen," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Of the participants at the weekend meeting of the Group of Seven financial leaders, only ECB President Wim Duisenberg appeared able and willing to use his policy tools to boost prospects for economic growth, saying the ECB "will not hesitate to act" if its assessment of the outlook for inflation changes. \par \par \uc2  At 0525 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y118.35, below Y118.75 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y119.01 late Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0736, lower than $1.0761 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.0765 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y127.04, down from Y127.79 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y128.11 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar then leaped around 30 pips to over Y118.60 on further media reports about the new BOJ leadership team. \par \par \uc2  Later Monday, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun and national broadcaster NHK reported Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has decided to name Fukui as the next BOJ governor. \par \par \uc2  The prime minister has also selected as deputy BOJ governor Toshiro Mutoh, former vice minister of finance, and Kazumasa Iwata, a senior official at the Cabinet Office who doubles as a professor at the University of Tokyo, the business daily reported. \par \par \uc2  Koizumi concluded that Fukui was the best choice in view of his rich experience in financial policy. \par \par \uc2  Fukui as BOJ chief would be "a non-event for the financial markets" as it is already factored in, said Goldman Sachs's Tetsufumi Yamakawa. The focus is likely to change to the dynamics between Fukui and the reported deputy Iwata. As a more radical proponent of monetary easing, it is uncertain what influence he could exert over policy meetings, given Fukui will have the full support of BOJ officials, said Yamakawa. \par \par \uc2  Aside from the naming of the BOJ team, factors influencing the yen in the near term include the possibility that Japanese monetary authorities will maintain their efforts to keep the yen weak amid recent signs that the nation's export-led recovery is slowing down. \par \par \uc2  A rising yen hurts Japan's exports, one of the main engines behind the nation's economic recovery, as it makes the country's goods more expensive in overseas markets and depresses Japanese firms' overseas earnings when repatriated. \par \par \uc2  Underscoring fears of potential yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi reiterated Monday the G7's statement that the G7 will "continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi's remarks helped to remind market participants that the G7 doesn't disapprove of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts, including stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to stem the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  Just last month, the MOF revealed that it secretly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market several times in mid- to late January, selling some Y700 billion for dollars through the Bank of Japan to curb speculative volatility. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.5772, down from $1.5796 in New York and lower than $1.6011 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or