\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Japan's G7 Snub Puts  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Focus Back On MOF, BOJ \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2 931 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2003\par 05:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  PARIS -(Dow Jones)- A Group of Seven meeting may have come and gone, but for Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players, the issues remain the same. \par \par \uc2  The lack of new G7 dialogue on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and general disinterest in Japan's grumbling about deflation and China's weak yuan leave Tokyo's focus right where it was last week: Who will be the next Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor? When will the Finance Ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  next? \par \par \uc2  An answer to the first question might not be far off. \par \par \uc2  The Yomiuri Shimbun and Kyodo News reported Monday that Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi is set to nominate former BOJ Deputy Governor and policy conservative Toshihiko Fukui as the next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor to replace Masaru Hayami, whose term ends March 19. \par \par \uc2  And in the hope of balancing Fukui's expected push for a steady policy framework, Koizumi will nominate University of Tokyo professor and Cabinet Office bureaucrat Kazumasa Iwata as one of the next two deputy governors at the BOJ, according to the reports. Iwata has previously advocated a more aggressive policy including adoption of an inflation target. \par \par \uc2  The dollar so far has slipped half a yen Monday on expectations that under Fukui, the BOJ won't aggressively ease policy. Some traders say confirmation of Fukui's appointment would put further upward pressure on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If it's true it's yen-positive," said Morgan Stanley \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Toru Umemoto. \par \par \uc2  At 0410 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y118.32. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, who discussed candidates for the BOJ's top job with Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi Sunday night, told reporters a decision would be made Monday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The next question is when the Finance Ministry will swoop in to stop the yen from rising. \par \par \uc2  The G7, though hardly vocal on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the weekend, might have provided an answer. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministers and central bankers, who wrapped up a two-day meeting in Paris Saturday, indicated they are happy with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's status quo by sticking to their well-worn stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate," they said in a joint statement, keeping their wording unchanged from their previous meeting in September. \par \par \uc2  For Japan, that statement could be interpreted as a G7 go-ahead for Tokyo's continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold back the yen if it surges too high against the dollar for Tokyo's liking. \par \par \uc2  "With the G7 wording unchanged, effectively, that means \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," said Morgan Stanley's Umemoto. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry, through a series of quiet yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the BOJ in mid- to late-January, put a floor under the dollar at Y117.50. \par \par \uc2  "That's still the level the market's going to focus on from here," Umemoto said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    G7 To Japan: You're On Your Own On Deflation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But more than anything, the market's attention will focus on the BOJ's\par new management team, especially as the G7 made clear Japan is on its own\par in fighting deflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It seems that Europe isn't that interested in deflation, nor is the\par U.S. Deflation appears to be something characteristic only of Japan,"\par Shiokawa said Saturday at a press conference following the G7 gathering.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That must have hurt, as Japan went into the Paris gathering with a\par hope its counterparts will acknowledge the risks of falling prices and\par the possibility that the phenomenon may spread to the rest of the world\par and hamper growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan also got little more than a lukewarm G7 response to its\par declaration that China should do more to liberalize its capital and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China's yuan is pegged to the dollar, and has recently depreciated\par against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in line with the U.S. dollar's broad-based\par weakness. Japan claims China, by keeping a dollar-peg, is exporting\par deflation through cheap exports.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Doing his best to spin things to his advantage, Shiokawa told\par reporters that he put forward the need for "countries belonging to the\par World Trade Organization", which includes China, to liberalize not only\par trade, but their capital and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There was considerable debate on the topic... and the (G7)\par understanding was that this should proceed," Shiokawa said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Not quite, according to U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, who said at\par a separate press conference: "That issue did not come up at any of my\par discussions."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That means it's up to Japan and Japan alone to do something to stop\par persistent prices falls - hence the focus on Japan's next top central\par banker.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ under Hayami hasn't ended deflation, despite anchoring\par short-term interest rates near zero and flooding the financial system\par with cash.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While Hayami promised the G7 in Paris - his last moment on the world\par stage - to "provide even more ample liquidity to the financial markets,"\par he has remained staunchly opposed to more radical reflation measures,\par such as setting a time-limit for achieving a set rate of inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Koizumi has played his cards close to his chest regarding Hayami's\par successor and has refused to reveal who he's considering for the post.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If reports of Fukui's appointment prove correct, the dollar could fall\par against the yen - possibly to Y117.50, according to Umemoto.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; +813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Arran Scott, Iain McDonald and Bennett Richardson\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030224dz2o0007o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
