\par\par \b \uc2  Mizoguchi says Japan to act on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  if needed. \b0\par\par\uc2 165 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2003\par 01:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 20 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Thursday there is no change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance that it will act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "We have seen some rapid moves recently. We believe that such rapid ups and downs are not appropriate," Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "We are closely monitoring the market from that standpoint." \par \par \uc2  He also said he saw no reason for the yen to rise given Japan's economic fundamentals compared with other major economies. \par \par \uc2  In early Tokyo trade the dollar was around 118.85 yen , compared to 118.70 in late New York trade. It hit a three-week low of 118.57 yen overnight. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in late January, and many wonder if such action will be discussed at a Group of Seven meeting in Paris this weekend. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030220dz2k009vq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
