\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Yamaguchi: Won't Pursue Cheap Yen Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2003\par 15:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- Neither the Bank of Japan nor the government have any intention of deliberately weakening the yen in an effort to boost growth via a surge in exports, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Yutaka Yamaguchi said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks in an effort to stop the yen appreciating against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Both the government of Japan and the Bank of Japan don't have any intention of pursuing a cheap yen policy to export our way out of our current difficulties," said Yamaguchi. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at the Royal Institute of International Affairs in London, Yamaguchi said that, given the "enormous flows" that pass through the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, any efforts to depreciate the yen would be futile. \par \par \uc2  A day before a meeting of central bankers and finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations takes place in Paris, Yamaguchi noted that a weak yen policy would also create major international political difficulties. \par \par \uc2  "The potential conflict with our trading partners would become rather serious, especially in the current global economic environment, in which growth in many areas is less than optimal." \par \par \uc2  The central banker said that a weaker yen policy might encourage Japanese companies to slow down restructuring efforts that are needed to restore their profitability. \par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi said Japanese consumers have proven surprisingly resilient in the face of falling nominal incomes, and added that while deflation will persist, it will ease over coming months. \par \par \uc2  "The most aggressive phase in the downward adjustment of prices appears to be over," he said. "Deflation appears to be decelerating in the months ahead." \par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi said the economy will grow by 0%-1% in the fiscal year that ends March 2004. \par \par \uc2  "The picture is not that encouraging," he said. \par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi is due to step down from his post at the Bank of Japan March 19. With the governor also being replaced on that date, there is speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may adopt new policies to tackle deflation. \par \par \uc2  But Yamaguchi believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do little more than give private companies time to restructure and return to profitability, chiefly by maintaining the stability of the financial system. \par \par \uc2  "The (\b \highlight22\uc2 central) bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to extend credit to any bank experiencing difficulties with systemic implications," he (Bank of Japan Deputy Governor Yutaka Yamaguchi) said. \par \par \uc2  With households averse to borrowing, and companies anxious to cut their debts rather than add to them, the Bank of Japan's zero-interest-rate policy - coupled with its injections of liquidity into the financial system - has its limits. \par \par \uc2  "Growth can alleviate or even solve some of the problems we are facing, even deflation," Yamaguchi said. "Monetary policy alone can't effectively induce higher economic growth." \par \par \uc2  Critics of the bank have suggested it move to targeting a specified inflation rate, in the hope that would encourage a self-fulfilling expectation that prices will rise. \par \par \uc2  But Yamaguchi said a move to inflation targeting would be dangerous, since given the limits of its monetary policy, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be promising something it would most likely be unable to deliver. \par \par \uc2  "By announcing a targeted rate of inflation we would be promising something to our people that we could not possibly deliver, and that's something we would hope to avoid," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's critics have also suggested it should start buying bonds denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way of injecting further liquidity into the economy. It already buys Japanese government bonds. \par \par \uc2  However, Yamaguchi said the bank couldn't pursue such a policy without the agreement of the government, since it would be interpreted by the international financial markets as an effort to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "If the Bank of Japan decided to aggressively conduct such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would be interpreted as a deliberate effort to weaken the yen," he said. "Therefore the Bank of Japan alone could not embark on such a policy option." \par \par \uc2  -Paul Hannon, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9491; paul.hannon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Corrected February 21, 2003, 12:56 PM (17:56 GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030220dz2k000wu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
