\par\par \b \uc2  Record US Trade Deficit Ups Stakes For Struggling Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2003\par 20:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The record U.S. December trade deficit underscores the rising challenge for the U.S. to attract foreign capital at a time when Iraq concerns and a sluggish economy are already weighing on investor inflows. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Thursday, the Commerce Department reported that the deficit in international trade in goods and services widened to $44.24 billion in December, up from a revised $40.00 billion in November. The December deficit was considerably more than the $38.50 billion figure that had been expected. \par \par \uc2  For all of 2002, the trade deficit reached a record $435.22 billion, a 21.5% increase from the 2001 deficit of $358.29 billion. \par \par \uc2  This has serious implications for the U.S.: the country needs to attract some $1.5 billion a day to fund its current  account deficit, which is largely comprised of trade in goods and services. With foreign direct investment into the U.S. having tailed off significantly, the U.S. relies largely on foreign purchases of stocks and bonds to fund its deficit. \par \par \uc2  But with the White House finding itself increasingly isolated in the court of world opinion for its tough stance towards Iraq, global distaste for risk is on the rise, making it more difficult for the world's largest economy to attract the funding to meet its deficit financing needs. \par \par \uc2  "The real issue is the U.S. certainly needs foreign capital at this point," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS Warburg in London. "But where is it going to find that capital at a time when risk aversion is high?" \par \par \uc2  If capital inflows were to dry up, the current account deficit would have to shrink, either through a slump in domestic demand or a fall in the dollar, or both. Not coincidentally, while the dollar typically weakens during the last month of the year, it took a particularly large tumble at the end of 2002, falling 5.1% against the euro in December. \par \par \uc2  In an environment of falling global risk appetite, investors are more reluctant to send investment capital abroad. As a result, the dollar tends to react negatively to  any developments that indicate that a U.S. led war in Iraq is likely to come soon, particularly if it appears the U.S. will not be backed by a broad coalition. \par \par \uc2  "We know the trend has been bad, and this is just more evidence that the trend is going to deteriorate" wherein the dollar is slammed by the double whammy of increasing trade deficits and greater risk aversion, said Alan Ruskin, research director for 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Cue The Twin Deficits\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the spot market, the dollar responded instantly to the latest trade\par data. It had been edging higher just prior to the release, with the euro\par trading near the day's low, at  $1.0755. But the trade data quickly\par reversed the greenback's direction, pushing the euro as high as $1.0832\par before it settled down to $1.0822 early in the New York afternoon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The trade data also focused attention on sluggish growth  in the U.S.\par Exports fell to $81.19 billion, driven by a sharp drop in overseas sales\par of capital goods. Capital goods  exports fell $2.22 billion for the\par month  to $22.19 billion, the lowest level since January 1997.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The trade deficit isn't the only shortfall the dollar has to worry\par about. The U.S. is also grappling with a mounting budget deficit, one\par that analysts believe could skyrocket if there is a war in Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Newly-appointed Treasury Secretary John Snow recently asked for the\par debt ceiling to be raised again, noted Jes Black, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst with\par MG Financial in New York, adding that "Over the longer-term, the dollar\par will remain vulnerable to further pressure due to the rising twin\par deficits," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par So far this year, the dollar has already fallen much further than many\par had expected, only recently finding some sort of support.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese officials have done their part to prop up  the dollar,\par revealing this month that they covertly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets\par in January in an effort to stem the yen's rise against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That also has implications for U.S. deficit financing: Jalinoos noted\par that the roughly $6 billion differential between expectations for the\par U.S. trade deficit in December and actual number was about the same\par amount of money that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par January.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While Snow has indicated that he isn't in favor of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it's likely that U.S. officials are pleased by the fact\par that the such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has helped to  fund the current account\par deficit, easing the dollar's sharp  downward projection.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts have speculated that the Japanese finance\par ministry has continued its covert \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in February, the\par resulting demand for U.S. assets from this official activity cannot be\par relied on, leaving the U.S. that much further from funding its widening\par deficits.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030220dz2k00a15\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
