\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slides as Iraq unease resurfaces. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2003\par 22:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 19 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Wednesday, tumbling to a three-week low against the yen as concerns about a possible war with Iraq resurfaced in markets after a brief respite. \par \par \uc2  Investors have sold the dollar on concern the United States could launch a unilateral military offensive against Iraq without the sanction of the United Nations. As Washington has tried without success to win the backing of reluctant allies, those fears have become more amplified. \par \par \uc2  Even as data showed the U.S. housing sector remained buoyant in January, both Wall Street and the dollar steadily lost ground throughout the session. Analysts attributed the data's lack of influence to the lingering uncertainty of Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "You still have to look at Iraq as the chief driver of exchange rates right now," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal in New York. \par \par \uc2  "It's very different to see the dollar pulling out of that morass without anything definitive that gives the Iraq situation more shape and more clearly defines the risk associated with it, especially with respect to U.S. asset markets," he added. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, U.N. diplomats said a joint U.S.-British effort to introduce a new resolution seeking authorization for war against Iraq may not be put to a vote before early March, after chief weapons inspector Hans Blix issues an update on weapons inspections. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bought 118.60 yen , its lowest level in three weeks and 0.27 percent lower on the day. Traders said fear that Japan could step into markets to sell its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped contain the dollar's losses. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been the primary beneficiaries of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening trend. In late U.S. trading, the euro traded at $1.0752 against the dollar , up 0.60 percent from its previous U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 1.3670 Swiss francs , down 0.65 percent from its previous U.S. close. Though sterling fell to a 3-1/2 year low against the euro at 67.38 pence , it rose 0.40 percent on the day against a broadly weaker U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , changing hands at $1.5963 . \par \par \uc2  JAPAN WARNINGS MOUNT, BUT MARKET IGNORES \par \par \uc2  During the Asian session, Japanese Vice Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi said the yen's jump of more than 1 percent on Tuesday had been "a bit rapid." He added that officials were closely watching market moves and would "take action if needed." \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi's comments dampened speculation that Japan had abandoned efforts to weaken the yen - which would make the country's exports more expensive in foreign markets - sparked when Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values would not be discussed at an upcoming Group of Seven finance ministers meeting in Paris. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi also said on Wednesday that current yen strength was not good for the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  "Being that Tokyo is not happy about the quick move in the last 48 hours against the dollar, I am not surprised by the talk in the market that there were bids by the (Bank of Japan)," said Vincent Palazzolo, chief dealer at SMBC. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it discretely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets several times in January, and talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has put investors on guard when the yen strengthens rapidly. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about the yen had given a deeply distracted market a pause from concerns about Iraq. Those concerns, however, returned after U.S. President George W. Bush said the United States would act without the backing of the United Nations if necessary. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 2104GMT. All data taken from Reuters\par calculated from the levels at 5:30 p.m. (22:30 GMT) in the\par previous New York session.\par                       Last  US Close    %      YTD     2002\par                              18 Feb.  Change %Change    Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par Euro/dlr             1.0745   1.0688   +0.53   +2.37   1.0496\par Dlr/yen              118.70   118.99   -0.24   -0.03   118.74\par Euro/yen             127.55   127.18   +0.29   +2.31   124.67\par Dlr/swiss            1.3674   1.3758   -0.61   -1.00   1.3812\par Stg/dlr              1.5963   1.5902   +0.38   -0.91   1.6110\par Dlr/cad              1.5120   1.5168   -0.32   -3.83   1.5722\par Aus/dlr              0.5940   0.5896   +0.75   +5.90   0.5609\par Euro/swiss           1.4696   1.4706   -0.07   +1.38   1.4496\par Euro/stg             0.6728   0.6717   +0.16   +3.32   0.6512\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par  Economic Forecasts...          Official rates...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......             Top events........\par  Diaries........... 