\par\par \b \uc2  'Mr Yen' expects new  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  tactic by  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima and Daiki Iga \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2003\par 11:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 14 (Reuters) - Japanese authorities, whose surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month has largely stabilised the yen, will likely try a new trick when they step into the market again, a noted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  guru said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Former Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke "Mr. Yen" Sakakibara said Tokyo may well try to overwhelm the market with sheer volume next time, despite its success with last month's discreetly conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We haven't had stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for about 10 years, so it was a surprise - and one key element for successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is surprise," Sakakibara told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "That means that stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't always be successful. The authorities are well aware of that, and next time they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think they will do it openly and on a much larger scale." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said earlier this month that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  discreetly on its behalf in the market on several occasions since mid-January, spending about $6 billion to buy dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had fallen to four-month lows around 117.40 yen  in mid-January, but has been drifting near 120 yen over the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Traders say a crucial goal of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to prevent the dollar from falling below 115 yen, given a break below that level would form a chart pattern known in the market as a "head-and-shoulders" formation. \par \par \uc2  Such a pattern would signal a major reversal of the dollar's rise to January 2002 peaks around 135 yen, and Japanese authorities have made little secret of their concern that a sharp rise in the yen would sidetrack Japan's export-driven economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Hiroshi Watanabe, who was appointed head of the MOF's international bureau last month, told reporters last week that preventing exchange rates from reaching certain levels was the most effective way of containing volatility. \par \par \uc2  The January 14 appointment of Zembei Mizoguchi as the new vice finance minister for international affairs has been cited as a key element in the new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics. But many traders also say Watanabe and Tatsuo Yamazaki, head of the MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division, were the masterminds behind the change. \par \par \uc2  REAL TEST \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, known as "Mr. Yen" for the influence he once wielded over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, declined to comment when asked if he thought Watanabe deserved to inherit the monicker, saying the real test was yet to come. \par \par \uc2  "Whether or not there is war in Iraq, I think the dollar's weakness will continue as a trend," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The weakness of Japan's own economy could weigh on the yen, but I think there will be situations where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be needed again." \par \par \uc2  For the time being, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably try to defend the 115 yen level against the dollar, an effort the United States would probably not oppose, although yen selling at levels beyond 120 would likely raise eyebrows, Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  He added that joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely, given newly appointed U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow appeared to dislike \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There is a capital flight out of the United States now, so they have no choice but to keep a strong dollar policy," he said. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar should fall sharply, I don't think they will make a big fuss about Japan trying to stop it. However, they're not going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  themselves because that's their philosophy." \par \par \uc2  Hence, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were unlikely to be a major topic when finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the Group of Seven leading economies meet in Paris next week. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a big topic. We're not in a situation where coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is needed, and Mr. Snow is not in favour of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030214dz2e00kjf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
