\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Edges Lower Vs Yen On Japan GDP Surprise \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Soble \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2003\par 06:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar slipped against the yen in Asia Friday after data showed Japan eked out modest economic growth in the fourth quarter last year, confounding expectations for the first contraction in a year. \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product grew a price-adjusted 0.5% from the previous quarter, an annualized 2.0% clip, the government said, as unexpectedly robust spending by companies and consumers added to revived overseas demand for Japanese goods. \par \par \uc2  Only one of the 25 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and Nikkei News had predicted a rise in GDP. On average, the analysts estimated the economy shrank 0.3% in the quarter. \par \par \uc2  The Valentine's Day surprise elicited only a mild reaction from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, however. The dollar fell less than half a yen, to a low for the day around Y120.28, before recovering steadily through mid-afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  The GDP data belied continued economic weakness in the world's second largest economy, as nominal GDP slipped 0.1% and only an adjustment for deep price falls kept the headline GDP figure in positive territory. And with the market focused on terrorism and a potential war in Iraq, a much bigger surprise would have been needed knock the dollar lower, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "No one's paying attention to Japanese fundamentals," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Instead, investors sat tight ahead of major U.S. economic data and a report by Hans Blix, the chief U.N. weapons inspector in Iraq, due later Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We could see more appetite (to trade) in London and New York" as those events unfold, Umemoto said. But he predicted the dollar would stick to a relatively narrow range of Y120.00-Y121.50 in the short term, as persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry support the pair while uncertainty over Iraq and threats of terrorism weigh on the upside. \par \par \uc2  The market was also little moved Friday by the Bank of Japan's decision to leave monetary policy unchanged, a verdict that had been widely expected. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y120.41, below Y120.61 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y120.95 late Thursday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0835, higher than $1.0822 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.0751 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y130.48, down slightly from Y130.52 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y130.03 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6216, up from $1.6189 in New York and higher than $1.6185 in Tokyo Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar's modest slide against the yen Friday, technical charts remained relatively bullish for the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hasn't formed a significant downtrend since slipping from its high for the week Tuesday, analysis shows, suggesting it could resume its short-term uptrend as soon as late in global trade Friday or early next week. \par \par \uc2  The bullish scenario could fade, however, if the U.N. weapons report knocks the dollar much lower. If the dollar breaks below Y120.20, an uptrend line from Jan. 27, a test of Y120.00 or even Y119.00 would become likely. \par \par \uc2  The possibility of a renewed decline in the dollar has kept Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  on alert. \par \par \uc2  The MOF's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  point man, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, warned markets Friday that Japan would take "appropriate steps as needed" to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility. \par \par \uc2  The ministry revealed Jan. 31 that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in January. The specter of more yen-selling has kept a floor under the greenback. \par \par \uc2  -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030214dz2e0008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
