\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slides on fears of terror attacks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2003\par 19:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 13 (Reuters) - The dollar fell more than 1 percent against the euro and the Swiss franc on Thursday on escalating fears over possible terror attacks against U.S. interests and those of its allies. \par \par \uc2  "There is a paranoia coursing through the market," said Hugh Walsh, vice president at Fortis U.S.A. in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  In Britain, flights were suspended out of Gatwick airport's north terminal near London and a Venezuelan man was arrested under anti-terrorism legislation after a live hand grenade was found in his baggage on arrival from Colombia. Two arrests also were made close to Heathrow, the country's other main airport. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, anti-aircraft missiles protected the Pentagon - one of the targets of the Sept. 11, 2001, attacks - and F-16 fighter jets were placed on 24-hour alert. There also was tightened security in New York City. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't look so good this morning.... People are worried that something could happen," said John Beerling, chief dealer at Wells Fargo in Minneapolis. \par \par \uc2  "It's disconcerting to watch tanks and guns rolling around Washington." \par \par \uc2  Concerns about terror attacks rose after the White House last Friday raised the nation's alert level to orange, the second highest. A taped message thought to be from Osama bin Laden, exhorting Muslims to attack U.S. interests, ratcheted up the perception of risk, since earlier messages from the al Qaeda leader were followed by attacks. \par \par \uc2  Walsh said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also were on edge ahead of of a report to the United Nations on Friday by chief weapons inspectors Hans Blix and Mohamed ElBaradei. \par \par \uc2  "There's a feeling that Blix will be more confrontational in this report, putting us closer to war," he said. \par \par \uc2  Independent experts have concluded that Iraq has missiles that exceed the limits of U.N. Security Council resolutions. But Iraqi Deputy Prime Minister Tareq Aziz on Thursday defended the weapons, saying their range modestly exceeded limits because they didn't have guidance systems. \par \par \uc2  North Korea also ruffled feathers when a senior official was quoted as saying the country was capable of striking U.S. targets anywhere in the world if provoked. On Wednesday, U.S. intelligence officials told the Senate that North Korea was developing missiles capable of hitting the U.S. West Coast, although Pyongyang had not tested the missiles. \par \par \uc2  By early afternoon in New York, the dollar was down about 1.2 percent against both the euro and the Swiss franc, trading at $1.0844 per euro and 1.3532 francs. \par \par \uc2  Other financial markets also succumbed to security fears. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average was off 1.3 percent while gold prices, which had been falling for days, jumped as safe-haven buying re-emerged. \par \par \uc2  RETAIL SALES ROBUST BUT NOT VERY HELPFUL TO DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  News that U.S. retail sales were better than expected in January gave the dollar a modest but temporary boost in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Overall retail sales fell 0.9 percent in January, a sharper than forecast drop, but rose 1.3 percent excluding autos, the fastest gain in over two years. \par \par \uc2  Stripping out both cars and gasoline, sales rose a robust 1.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  The market had been expecting a modest 0.5 percent rise in sales excluding cars, so the strong number briefly gave the dollar a lift. But the greenback quickly drifted as attention turned back to global tensions. \par \par \uc2  "The economic fundamentals are so overshadowed by geopolitical events that the market is not going to do anything with this data," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. \par \par \uc2  Economic news from Europe was uninspiring, but dealers said it did not attract much attention in a market fixated on global security concerns. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its monthly bulletin on Thursday that growth risks in the euro zone remain tilted toward a slowdown, adding that it was almost impossible to judge the economic and monetary policy implications of a possible war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the head of Germany's engineering employers' association said signs were emerging that export trade was beginning to wobble in the face of euro gains. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan began a two-day policy-setting meeting on Thursday, but the market expects no change in policy. \par \par \uc2  By early afternoon in New York, the dollar was down 0.65 percent against the yen at 120.42 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen has been lagging down moves in the dollar/European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  admitted to yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030213dz2d00zhx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
