\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. manufacturers meet Snow, discuss dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2003\par 21:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Feb 12 (Reuters) - A leading group of U.S. manufacturers met Treasury Secretary John Snow on Wednesday and took questions from the group's members about the value of the dollar, the group said. \par \par \uc2  "Newly confirmed Treasury Secretary John Snow met with NAM (National Association of Manufacturers) members for a candid, no-media meeting," NAM said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "The secretary took questions posed by the audience, and addressed issues such as the value of the dollar." \par \par \uc2  NAM has been spearheading a drive to persuade the administration to abandon its "strong dollar" policy. Even though the dollar has weakened in recent months against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the manufacturers argue that it is still overvalued on a trade-weighted basis and is therefore preventing the manufacturing sector from recovering. \par \par \uc2  Manufacturing was the sector of the U.S. economy hardest hit by the recession that started in 2001. Some recent manufacturing data have shown signs of improvement. The Institute of Supply Management's survey of U.S. manufacturing activity came in at 53.9 in January, just above expectations and the third month the number was over 50.0 - the barrier that separates expansion from contraction. \par \par \uc2  But the Labor Department's payrolls report for the same month showed the manufacturing sector continued to shed jobs. \par \par \uc2  NAM's latest tactic is to try to get U.S. officials to speak out against countries NAM claims artificially manipulate their exchange rates. NAM cites China, which has a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg backed up by vast \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in January to try to weaken the yen, as examples. \par \par \uc2  NAM's statement said Snow, a former railroad executive, spoke of the downturn in manufacturing since 2000 and of the need for policies to boost manufacturing. \par \par \uc2  But NAM's pleas on the dollar so far appear to have fallen on deaf ears. In his confirmation hearing in January, Snow repeated the "strong dollar" line that has been used by both the Bush and Clinton administrations. \par \par \uc2  "I favor a strong dollar," Snow said. "A strong dollar is in the national interest." \par \par \uc2  In reaction to questions from Reuters about the Snow meeting, a spokesman for the Treasury Department said Snow made his views on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  clear at the confirmation hearing. \par \par \uc2  "Anyone who wants to know his position can read those comments," the spokesman said. "Otherwise, we have no intention to react to every comment or question about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs, John Taylor, has said China should move to a more flexible exchange rate. But he said it is up to China to decide when to do so. \par \par \uc2  Fired White House economic advisor Lawrence Lindsey said last month U.S. officials should convince China to get rid of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, as the peg makes it hard for manufacturers to compete. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030212dz2c0132d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
