\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar calm as Washington allies wrangle over war. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2003\par 12:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 10 (Reuters) - The dollar trod water against the yen and euro on Monday as divisions deepened between the United States and NATO allies over a war against Iraq, overshadowing strong U.S. jobs data last week. \par \par \uc2  The battered greenback jumped against the euro on Friday on news that U.S. non-farm payrolls surged in January at the fastest pace in two years, but has sunk back on worries over U.S. and British isolation over how to force Iraq to disarm. The main political focus for the week is Friday's report to the U.N. Security Council by U.N. weapons inspectors, but markets were already on edge on Monday as rifts between Washington and its key allies appeared to deepen. \par \par \uc2  On the economic front, testimony by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan before congressional committees on Tuesday and Wednesday will be closely watched for signs of the current Fed assessment of the limping U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "We see a bit of effort to consolidate around $1.08 and all eyes are on Greenspan, looking for signs whether the economy is moving out of the 'soft spot' or is still in it," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Markets will want to gauge if the Fed is even contemplating a new rate cut, which might heighten concern that the economy is not finding any form of growth." \par \par \uc2  At 1107 GMT the dollar was unchanged from late Friday's levels in New York at $1.0813 per euro , around a cent above a recent four-year low. \par \par \uc2  On the yen it was also barely changed at 120.30 yen . The euro was also steady at 130.20 yen . \par \par \uc2  U.S. ALLIES WEIGH OPTIONS \par \par \uc2  The dollar was unmoved by wrangling within NATO on Monday morning as Germany, France and Belgium vetoed the military alliance's planning for measures to defend Turkey in the event of a U.S.-led war on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The vetoes appeared to heighten Washington's isolation in its calls to disarm Iraq quickly, in the face of opposition to swift military action from other NATO members. \par \par \uc2  They were seen as reflecting the NATO members' concerns that they would undermine diplomatic efforts to disarm Iraq without force. \par \par \uc2  "This increases the likelihood that America will go in alone against Iraq which is probably the worst of all scenarios," said a trader at a British bank. "But the dollar hasn't moved much because the NATO meetings are still ongoing so we're waiting to see what happens." \par \par \uc2  NATO is due to reconvene at 1530 GMT on Monday after Turkey formally sought consultations in the alliance after the vetoes. \par \par \uc2  Also on Monday Russian President Vladmir Putin holds talks with French President Jacques Chirac after visiting Germany at the weekend, when he and German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said they hoped Iraq could be disarmed without war. Germany, currently chairing the Security Council, maintains it sees no need for a second U.N. resolution authorising force against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, chief U.N. inspector Hans Blix said in Athens on his way from Iraq, where he reviewed new evidence from Baghdad on its weapons arsenal that he saw no new proof concerning the Iraqi arsenal. \par \par \uc2  "In the very short-term the dollar can swing both ways on comments on pro-and anti-second resolution which are likely to come out ahead of Friday," said a European bank trader. \par \par \uc2  "We are also keeping an eye on U.S. confidence data this week since data out of the U.S. have surprised on the upside." \par \par \uc2  U.S. data late this week includes February consumer sentiment, January retail sales and industrial output. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN TO TAKE A BREAK \par \par \uc2  The dollar struggled to extend gains in Tokyo as Japanese exporters sold the greenback and Japanese investors were rumoured to be interested in converting income gains on U.S. Treasuries into yen, a move seen last week. \par \par \uc2  Japanese markets are closed on Tuesday but nervousness over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities is weighing on the yen, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned two weeks ago it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi said on Monday that Japan was closely watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent an overshoot or volatility in exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  On the economic data front, figures showed Japan's export performance stayed strong in December. \par \par \uc2  But bank lending fell for the 61st month in a row and the growth of money in circulation slowed. That trend has raised calls for the government to replace outgoing Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami with someone who will take more drastic monetary easing steps. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has said he will decide on a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor around February 20, but top government spokesman Yasuo Fukuda said on Monday he may not be named until after a Group of Seven meeting on February 21-22. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030210dz2a00m8k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
