\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar pauses, UN inspectors, Greenspan hold key. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2003\par 10:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 10 (Reuters) - The dollar trod water against the yen and the euro on Monday, as investor uncertainty regarding divisions between U.N. Security Council members on a war against Iraq overshadowed strong U.S. labour market data last week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped on Friday on news that U.S. non-farm payrolls surged in January at the fastest pace in two years but has since sunk back on concerns over U.S.-UK isolation among permanent Security Council members over how to force Iraq to disarm. The main political focus for the week is Friday's report to the U.N. Security Council by chief U.N. weapons inspectors but top level diplomacy between Russia, France and Germany on means of disarming Iraq peacefully will set the stage in the run-up. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan testifies before congressional committees on Tuesday and Wednesday, which in normal circumstances would be the market highlight for the week. \par \par \uc2  "It's going to be a Blix and Greenspan double act this week," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "Ahead of Greenspan and Blix, markets are going to be cautious and hesitate to take too many positions, although it does still seem the dollar has found its legs a little bit this morning." \par \par \uc2  By 0900 GMT the dollar was unchanged from late Friday's levels in New York at $1.0818 per euro , around a cent above a recent four-year low. \par \par \uc2  On the yen it was also practically unchanged at 120.35 yen , while the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also steady at 130.21 yen . \par \par \uc2  ROUNDS OF DIPLOMACY \par \par \uc2  Russian President Vladmir Putin visits France on Monday and holds talks with President Jacques Chirac after visiting Germany at the weekend, when he and German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder both said they hoped Iraq could be disarmed peacefully. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush kept up pressure both on Baghdad and the U.N. Security Council at the weekend, saying the U.N. must move quickly or be deemed irrelevant. \par \par \uc2  UN weapons inspectors meanwhile said in Iraq at the weekend they saw "the beginning of a change of heart on the part of Iraq" in cooperating with inspectors but more needed to be done. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a lack of strong international support for a U.S.-led war could be negative for the greenback and all eyes will be on the inspectors' assessment of Iraqi cooperation on Friday and what support there is for a second UN resolution on disarming Baghdad. \par \par \uc2  "In the very short-term the dollar can swing both ways on comments on pro-and anti-second resolution which are likely to come out ahead of Friday," said a European bank trader. \par \par \uc2  "We are also keeping an eye on U.S. confidence data this week since data out of the U.S. have surprised on the upside." \par \par \uc2  U.S. data at the latter end of this week includes February consumer sentiment, January retail sales and industrial production, with Greenspan speaking beforehand. \par \par \uc2  "Greenspan is likely to be modestly upbeat and unlikely to cause too much concern in the market or indicate a further cut in rates, although geopolitical concerns will be paramount," Kotecha said. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN TO TAKE A BREAK \par \par \uc2  The dollar struggled to extend gains in Tokyo as Japanese exporters sold the greenback and Japanese investors were rumoured to be interested in converting income gains on U.S. Treasuries into yen, a move seen last week. \par \par \uc2  Japanese markets are closed on Tuesday but nervousness over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities is weighing on the yen, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned two weeks ago it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi said on Monday that Japan was closely watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent an overshoot or volatility in exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  On the economic data front, figures showed Japan's export performance stayed strong in the last month of 2002. \par \par \uc2  But on a bleaker note, bank lending fell for the 61st month in a row and the amount of money in circulation slowed. That trend has raised calls for the government to replace outgoing Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami with someone who will take more drastic monetary easing steps. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has said he will decide on a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor around February 20, but top government spokesman Yasuo Fukuda said on Monday he may not be named until after a Group of Seven meeting on February 21-22. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030210dz2a00i86\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
