\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dlr pares post-US jobs data gains, Iraq in focus. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2003\par 18:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 7 (Reuters) - The dollar received a fleeting boost against the euro on Friday on euphoria over a constructive U.S. employment report, but the gains quickly gave way to the specter of war and an elevated terror threat. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been encumbered by weakness in the economy and by global tensions. The possibility of U.S. war with Iraq remains the focus of market activity. \par \par \uc2  Economic concerns and dollar selling were temporarily allayed by news that the U.S. economy added 143,000 jobs in January, the fastest rate in more than two years. The jobless rate fell to 5.7 percent from 6.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said a closer look at the data revealed that they may have been distorted by temporary factors, prompting traders to snatch away the dollar's hard-fought gains. \par \par \uc2  "The headline number looked strong but as it was in December (it was) extremely misleading," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The retail sector did far less hiring in December, and so they did far less firing in January," he said. "Therefore, on a seasonally adjusted basis the pattern looks more volatile as a result - a total head-fake." \par \par \uc2  Interest to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also dampened by news that the U.S. government was raising its color-coded threat assessment to the second-highest level of security alert. That prompted safe-haven flows into European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  By midday U.S. trade, the euro had reversed course to trade near $1.0810 , well above its post-jobs-data spike lower to $1.0725 and unchanged from its prior U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar was well supported against the Japanese yen, trading around 120.35 yen , up 0.4 percent on the day. Traders expect the dollar's advance to be capped near 120.50 region. \par \par \uc2  The market remained mindful of Japan's displeasure with a strong yen, as Japanese authorities acknowledged last week they had sold yen for dollars to temper a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation that threatens the country's economic growth. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar clung to modest gains against the Swiss franc  around 1.3565\par francs. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pressed sterling lower to $1.6300       ,\par which was hampered in London trading by grim British manufacturing\par data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WAR WORRIES DAMPEN GAINS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, a day after U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell addressed\par the United Nations Security Council, President George W. Bush accused\par Iraqi President Saddam Hussein of making dilatory maneuvers to prolong\par inspections and drive a wedge between Washington and members of the\par Security Council.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Though Bush has displayed a willingness to seek U.N. authorization for\par a military strike, he warned Iraq that "the game is over," leaving\par market observers to conclude that armed conflict is all but\par inevitable.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The (jobs) number was marginally better than the trend of the last\par couple of months, but it's not enough to change opinions sufficiently\par given what is going to happen with the U.N. and the war," said a dealer\par with a large European bank in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tensions were also escalating in North Korea as the United States said\par it was ready for any contingency after the communist state issued\par threats of preemptive strike against U.S. forces and suggested it was\par poised to restart the atomic reactor at the heart of its nuclear arms\par program.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Japan, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa and Vice Finance Minister\par Zembei Mizoguchi both said they were closely watching the market and\par would take appropriate action as needed. Mizoguchi declined to comment\par on market talk that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell yen on\par Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers have treated the yen gingerly in recent sessions, as Japan has\par stiffened its resolve to keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weak.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There has been very little talk of Japanese repatriation of assets,\par which is generally the theme this time of year as we get into the March\par 31 year-end," a shift that increases demand for yen, said Scott Shultz,\par trader at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "Given the North Korean\par situation, that's not going to help the yen either as the situation\par remains tense."\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 1651GMT. All data taken from Reuters\par calculated from the levels at 5:30 p.m. (22:30 GMT) in the\par previous New York session.\par                       Last  US Close    %      YTD     2002\par                              06 Feb.  Change %Change    Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par Euro/dlr             1.0803   1.0822   -0.18   +2.92   1.0496\par Dlr/yen              120.38   119.85   +0.44   +1.38   118.74\par Euro/yen             130.06   129.69   +0.29   +4.32   124.67\par Dlr/swiss            1.3578   1.35