\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar claws higher, market awaits U.S. jobs data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2003\par 08:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 7 (Reuters) - The dollar was a shade firmer on Friday ahead of key U.S. jobs data that will offer a distraction, if only temporarily, to a market wary about a potential war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  January jobs figures are due at 1330 GMT and economists in a Reuters survey forecast on average that 70,000 new jobs were created compared with a loss of 101,000 jobs in December. \par \par \uc2  The unemployment rate is seen at 6.0 percent, unchanged from December. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. jobs report is the main focus for now, though we expect only a temporary reaction in the market, if at all, and attention may just shift back to developments over Iraq," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Shinsei Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had hit four-year lows versus the euro and the Swiss franc earlier this week on mounting war fears as the market awaited U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell's speech before the U.N Security Council on the U.S. case against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  But short-covering in the dollar has since kept the greenback resilient, and traders said further position-adjustments were taking place before the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the market's reaction to Powell, you could say that it has factored in any war in Iraq," said Kazunari Kobayashi, treasury manager at State Street Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The euro was sold after Powell's presentation, one of the highlights of the ongoing story about Iraq." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By late afternoon in Tokyo, the euro was giving up ground at $1.0792/93\par  from $1.0827 in late New York trade. Against the yen, the single\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eased to 129.42/51 yen            compared with 129.74 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was also higher against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 119.92/97\par yen  from 119.83 yen in late New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders took in stride Thursday's comments by U.S. President George W.\par Bush that he would welcome a new U.N. resolution authorising war on\par Iraq, while at the same time signaling the United States was ready to\par attack with or without U.N. backing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par YEN HAMPERED\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen was hampered by a variety of factors, including wariness about\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said that it secretly stepped into the market\par late last month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen has also been curtailed by Japanese investors who have tired of\par low interest rates at home and have turned to buying foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think it is wrong to think the yen was capped simply because of\par wariness about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said State Street Bank's Kobayashi.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Japanese investors are investing abroad, in the euro and the\par Australian dollar. Such fund flows are also playing a part," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Foreign investors have also been selling Japanese shares, said Hidehiko\par Inamura, vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales at Citibank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Sales of Japanese shares by North American investors soared this week.\par Such flows of real money will cap the yen," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  enjoyed some respite against European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the day, with profit-taking hitting the sterling/yen\par cross  after a surprise rate cut by the Bank of England on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank trimmed interest rates a quarter of a percentage point to 3.75\par percent, its first cut since November 2001, indicating growing concerns\par about the global economy and recent falls in the stock market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...        Debt...      Indicators...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par  Official rates...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......             Top events........\par  Diaries...........        Diaries Index........\par  Press Digests....         Polls on G7 economies..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030207dz2700dl2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
