\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's ninja tactics on yen seen as temporary fix. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2003\par 08:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 7 (Reuters) - Japan's new tactic of stemming the yen's rise by stealth has proved successful so far, but analysts say aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will also be needed in the future for such action to remain effective. \par \par \uc2  Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  revealed a week ago that it had quietly bought $6 billion worth of dollars for yen from mid-January, traders have been edgy about buying the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The new style has the market on its toes," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at JP Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said that effect will soon run out unless monetary authorities conduct large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they have done in the past. \par \par \uc2  "The biggest fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that people will lose money," said Hideo Kumano, a senior economist at Dai-ichi Life Research Institute \par \par \uc2  "Unless they conduct aggressive large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some point in the future, the effects will run out because such action is what players are afraid of." \par \par \uc2  In recent years, the MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  dramatically and in large amounts, immediately confirming such actions, and having a noticeable impact on rates. \par \par \uc2  But January's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first under newly appointed Vice Finance Minister Zembei Mizoguchi, was not revealed until the end of the month. Mizoguchi has stressed that the purpose was to prevent volatility and not to push down the yen. \par \par \uc2  A senior MOF official said on Thursday there was no set rule on whether it should immediately announce such actions, and it depended on the circumstances. \par \par \uc2  Also, the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thought to have been done by placing "leave orders" - instructions to buy or sell at a certain level - to cause rates to "hold", rather than aggressively seeking to push up the dollar by continuing to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it rises, as was the case under Mizoguchi's immediate predecessors Haruhiko Kuroda and Eisuke Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  BEST CHOICE NOW \par \par \uc2  So far, the new approach is proving effective. \par \par \uc2  "If you see a bid that could be from the BOJ, it'll be hard to sell (the dollar)," said JP Morgan's Tanase. "As long as the MOF doesn't reveal the level at which it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this will be effective." \par \par \uc2  Dai-ichi Life's Kumano said covert action was the best tactic for the ministry now, given the dollar's limited upside due to pressure from the possibility of a U.S.-led war against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "Since the dollar is now weighed down by war worries, it would be hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be effective in the long term," he said. "I think covert action was the best choice given such circumstances." \par \par \uc2  Other analysts said the stealth approach was the best way for MOF to avoid criticism from other countries that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has drawn criticism from trading partners such as China and South Korea, since a weaker yen would make it more difficult to sell their products in Japan. \par \par \uc2  New U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow says he favours a strong dollar but also that he favours an open \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Analysts think the MOF was particularly keen to stem the yen's rise ahead of the end of the fiscal year on March 31. Any sign that the economy was hurtling towards a recession could hit the stock market and exacerbate banks' bad loan problems. \par \par \uc2  Officials also fear that a strong yen could hurt Japan's exports, one of the few bright spots in an economy that some say is close to entering its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030207dz2700dff\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
