\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar eases as global leaders debate Iraq moves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2003\par 23:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 6 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Thursday as it became increasingly clear that the United States had failed to rally support for a more forceful disarmament of Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose sharply on Wednesday after U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell presented evidence to the U.N. Security Council showing Baghdad had concealed elements of its weapons program. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar drifted late on Wednesday and into Thursday as other veto-wielding members of the Security Council called for more time for weapons inspectors to do their work. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday perhaps the market thought Powell would present enough evidence to sway more support, but it doesn't look like he hastened the naysayers to join the U.S. side, so the dollar's up move has stagnated and it has begun to fall," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York. \par \par \uc2  Chief weapons inspectors Hans Blix and Mohamed ElBaradei, who have warned Baghdad that time was running out for it to cooperate fully, will visit Iraq from Feb. 8 to 10. They will report back to the Security Council on Feb. 14. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Iraq said the arms inspectors had held their first private interview with an Iraqi scientist. Private interviews have been a major sticking point between the inspectors and Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Baghdad has not given away anything on the other major demand by the inspectors, surveillance flights. \par \par \uc2  The dollar trimmed its losses late in the session after the White House reported that President George W. Bush planned later on Thursday to reiterate his willingness for a second U.N. resolution authorizing military force in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  At the briefing, Bush said the nation would support a new resolution "that makes clear the Security Council stands behind its previous demands." \par \par \uc2  By late afternoon in New York, the euro was trading at $1.0832 , up 0.44 percent on the session. The dollar was off 0.47 percent against the Swiss franc at 1.3536 francs . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said euro gains were limited by talk of selling by mostly European central banks in the $1.0855-65 area, near the day's highs. \par \par \uc2  While most of the focus was on Iraq, dealers also took note of the increasingly frayed relations between the United States and North Korea. The United States on Thursday shrugged off Pyongyang's warning that it could preemptively strike U.S. forces as "nothing new." The White House also said it was ready to deal with any contingencies in North Korea. \par \par \uc2  EURO GAINS ON STERLING \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday did what the market expected and left its key interest rate unchanged at 2.75 percent at its policy board meeting. \par \par \uc2  But the Bank of England surprised traders with a 25 basis point cut in its interest rate to 3.75 percent, a 48-year low. \par \par \uc2  Dealers bought euros for Sterling aggressively on the move on the basis of Sterling's narrowing yield advantage. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose 0.61 percent against the pound to 66.09 pence . Against the dollar, Sterling eased 0.19 percent to $1.6376 . \par \par \uc2  Data released on Thursday painted a grim picture of Germany's economy, with manufacturing orders falling 4.1 percent in December, the biggest drop in 7 years. \par \par \uc2  Taken together with news earlier this week of a sharp spike in German unemployment, dealers said the figures reminded the market of the slow pace of the recovery in Germany, the euro zone's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  "The economic news out of Europe has been deteriorating, in Germany in particular, and the ECB's reluctance to cut rates ... will be a long-term problem for the euro," said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the United States will release its most closely watched economic indicator, non-farm payrolls. Economists polled by Reuters expect the data to show 70,000 new jobs were added to the economy in January after a fall of 101,000 in December. The jobless rate is expected to hold at 6 percent. \par \par \uc2  BOJ WATCH \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped 0.13 percent against the yen to 119.80 yen . Dealers again cited fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too quickly. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  quietly sold some $6 billion worth of yen in January. \par \par \uc2  "You can't discount the talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might still be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  given that they bought six yards of dollars last month," said Hugh Walsh, vice president at Fortis U.S.A. He added that there was speculation Japan was trying to get the dollar to make higher highs against the yen, thereby manufacturing a technical signal to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030207dz26001x4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
