\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Mizoguchi - Happy  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mkt got  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  message. \b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2003\par 02:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat said on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was happy to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market take the message from its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month. \par \par \uc2  "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on the view that it is inappropriate for the market to overshoot or gyrate too much. So it is a good thing that our intention is understood," Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The MOF has said it had the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from mid-January, spending about $6 billion to buy dollars for yen. A MOF official said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at stabilising the yen and not simply pushing it down. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which had fallen to four-month lows below 117.50 yen in mid-January, was trading slightly above 120 yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials worry that excessive yen strength could harm exports and derail a faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the euro's recent strength could become a topic at the Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting later this month, Mizoguchi declined to comment and said it was a problem for the European authorities. \par \par \uc2  The euro briefly rose to four-year highs against the dollar above $1.09 and 3 = year highs against the yen above 130 yen on Wednesday before retreating in a nervous market that is on its toes amid worries about a U.S.-led military attack in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi declined to comment on the market's reaction to a statement by U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell to the U.N. Security Council on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar steadied in early Asian trading on Thursday after surging in U.S. trade on the back of what many traders considered to be a convincing speech on Iraq by Powell. \par \par \uc2  It had staged a comeback from four-year lows against the euro and the Swiss franc in U.S. trading as traders bought it back, believing Powell had made a strong case for war that would convince other major countries to join the cause. \par \par \uc2  But it pared some of those gains as it became clear that other members of the Security Council - including veto-wielding France, Russia and China - remained wary. \par \par \uc2  Powell revealed audio tapes and satellite photos which he said buttressed the U.S. view that Iraqi President Saddam Hussein had concealed weapons of mass destruction. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030206dz260025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
