\par\par \b \uc2  Mizoguchi - happy  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market got  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  message. \b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2003\par 01:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat said on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was happy to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market take the message from its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month. \par \par \uc2  "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on the view that it is inappropriate for the market to overshoot or gyrate too much. So it is a good thing that our intention is understood," Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters. Asked if the euro's recent strength could become a topic at the Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting later this month, Mizoguchi declined to comment and said it was a problem for the European authorities. \par \par \uc2  The MOF has said it had the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from mid-January, spending about $6 billion to buy dollars for yen. A MOF official said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at stabilising the yen and not simply pushing it down. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials worry that excessive yen strength could harm exports and derail a faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030206dz260018l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
