\par\par \b \uc2  BOE Cut Seen As Hint Weak Dollar Is Irking Central Banks \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2003\par 18:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of England's surprise 25 basis-point rate cut Thursday indicates that flagging major economies are having to take steps in an effort to mitigate the pressure on their economies from a sagging dollar, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts reckon. \par \par \uc2  The BOE's move, which took many economists aback, reflects "broader dollar weakness (that) is putting pressure on other governments and central banks to stimulate  internal demand," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The prospects for demand, both globally and domestically, are somewhat weaker than previously anticipated," the BOE stated in its statement that accompanied the reduction of its key short term lending rate  to 3.75%. \par \par \uc2  The vast majority of economists had anticipated the BOE holding steady, due to the still strong domestic housing market and sturdy consumer sector in the U.K. But in easing now, the bank clearly placed more emphasis on the possibility of future economic weakness, than current domestic economic strength. The BOE may also have been acting to check the pace of capital flows that have boosted sterling, some analysts think. \par \par \uc2  Part of the reason for the dollar's descent after the Federal Reserve cut rates very aggressively in 2001 and 2002  to a four-decade low of 1.25%, has been a rising tide of fixed-income flows shifting out of dollar-denominated assets  into higher-yielding sovereigns elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  One way of slowing those flows - and taking the edge off  other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ascent - is for rival central banks to slash their own lending rates, making their respective governments' fixed-income securities less attractive to global bond investors. \par \par \uc2  The key \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that some analysts believe may be forced to try to check its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise is the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ECB's minimum bid rate stands at 2.75%, after the bank cut rates for the first time in more than a year in December. Earlier Thursday, the ECB kept rates on hold, while ECB officials said that the pace and the extent of the euro's recent sharp gains against the dollar aren't a  concern. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, with joblessness in Germany, Europe's biggest economy, now at a troubling 11% and widespread pain being felt in both the manufacturing and the financial sector, there's a gathering sense that "the stronger euro is creating some economic pain," said Joseph Quinlan, Global Economist with Johns Hopkins University in Washington. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par       The War Factor\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro, which in January topped $1.09 for the first time in more\par than three years, has risen nearly 10% over the  past two months,\par effectively offsetting the impact of the ECB's December rate cut, some\par economists argue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Should a war erupt in Iraq, adding pressure on global central banks to\par attempt to insulate their respective economies against shocks emanating\par from such a conflict, the  ECB is seen as even more likely to ease,\par strategists said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The risk to the U.S. of playing a leading role in any attack on Iraq's\par Saddam Hussein could steepen the dollar's losses, making the going even\par harder for non-U.S. exporters to sell their goods competitively abroad.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Europe's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conundrum is just one piece in a widening global\par pattern. Over the past year, more central banks have become increasingly\par willing to actively weaken their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , by hook or by crook, points\par out Larry Kantor, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy with JP Morgan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since the dollar embarked on its sustained descent against its major\par counterparts, starting in February 2002, it has shed some 19% against\par the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the dollar's decline has been generalized, sparking increasingly\par intense reactions from a growing coterie of central banks."There are an\par increasing number of countries that don't want their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par appreciate," Kantor said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to check the\par ascent of the yen in late January, and prior to  that in May and June of\par last year, the Swiss National Bank has signaled it is also prepared to\par act.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par       Swiss Also Concerned\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After making a series of aggressive rate cuts over the past two years,\par in late January SNB officials began preparing the markets for the\par likelihood that they will take  concrete steps to prevent their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par from rising too much - in this case against the euro. SNB President\par Jean-Pierre Roth said that the bank is prepared to cut rates  further\par and to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if the franc's strength\par poses a risk to growth. The Swiss franc  is a classic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of refuge\par in times of war and has also gained sharply against the dollar as\par prospects of war have loomed larger.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In addition, the latest flurry of activity by Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par whose massive rate cuts and new restrictions on two-week deposits last\par month were designed to check the forint's rise against the euro, sent\par shock waves through markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But it is the efforts of central banks to mitigate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par appreciation against the dollar that may elicit the  most high profile\par concern, among policy makers, financial markets and U.S. manufacturers,\par analysts anticipate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The more that (central banks and governments) manipulate and manage\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  globally, the more that puts pressure to respond" on newly\par appointed Treasury Secretary John Snow. Quinlan said Snow will likely be\par under increasing pressure from U.S. exporters to keep the dollar\par sufficiently soft to boost their competitive edge, said Quinlan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Snow, who took office this week, has already indicated however, his\par aversion to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030206dz26004s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
