\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar at four-year lows ahead of Powell speech. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2003\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to four-year lows against the euro and the Swiss franc on Wednesday ahead of U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell's attempt to persuade the world that war with Iraq is justified. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that dollar sentiment remained bearish ahead of Powell's address before the U.N. Security Council at 1530 GMT, when he will reveal what Washington says is evidence that Iraq is hiding banned weapons of mass destruction and obstructing U.N. arms inspectors. \par \par \uc2  "Gold, short-term U.S. Treasuries and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are being bought and there's only one reason: everybody is conscious of a possible war," said Kota Kimura, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0650 GMT the euro was fetching $1.0935 its highest level since March 1999, after triggering stop-loss buying around $1.0910. It was quoted at $1.0890 in late U.S. trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro's rise against the dollar pushed the European unit to a 3-= year high of 130.53 yen \par \par \uc2  The safe-haven Swiss franc was at a four-year peak of 1.3410 against the dollar. It fetched 1.3455 franc in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 119.31 yen compared with 119.68 yen. \par \par \uc2  Gold prices rose to levels not seen in more than six years as investors continued to seek safe havens. Oil prices also gained more than two percent on supply concerns in the event of war. \par \par \uc2  POWELL UNDER SPOTLIGHT \par \par \uc2  The market's focus is on whether Washington will be able to persuade sceptical allies such as France, Germany and Russia that an attack against Iraq is crucial for the international community. \par \par \uc2  "It's not yet clear if the U.S. will go to war after seeking another resolution by the U.N. Security Council," said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I think the euro will remain favoured. It will reach $1.10 sooner or later," he predicted. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush hinted last week that the United States could attack Iraq even without a U.N. resolution. Traders think the dollar will be damaged if Bush chooses to do so. \par \par \uc2  "The best scenario for the dollar would be if the United States gathers international support, quickly launches an attack and finishes it up soon," said a spot trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "Unless this happens, it might be difficult for the dollar to regain strength again," he said. \par \par \uc2  Investors worry that a long-lasting war would jeopardise the fragile U.S. economic recovery, trigger a spike in oil prices and put the brakes on corporate profit growth. \par \par \uc2  The bulk of the fourth-quarter reporting season is over with few signs that U.S. corporate earnings are poised for a solid upturn in 2003. \par \par \uc2  Weakness on Wall Street discourages fund inflows to the Uniteds States from the rest of the world and burdens the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Still, traders said the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep the dollar supported, at least against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry revealed last week that the Bank of Japan had quietly sold yen for dollars in the second half of January. Japan is worried that a strong yen is hurting exports and could derail a faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030206dz2500ba0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
