\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Strategists see weak yen against dlr in coming year. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Joanne Russell \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2003\par 13:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 5 (Reuters) - Japanese policymakers will continue to talk down the yen in the coming year which should push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a little lower against the dollar, according to a Reuters poll published on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  This month's poll of 50 analysts and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists, carried out February 3 to 5, gave a mid-range forecast for the yen  at 120.00 per dollar in one month's time. \par \par \uc2  Analysts then saw the yen at 122.00 per dollar in three, six and 12 months' time. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly the domestic economy is weak and the Japanese authorities don't want to see the yen strengthen any further," said Julian Jessop at Standard Chartered in London. \par \par \uc2  "Overall policymakers will be happy to try and stabilise the yen round about current levels which is why we've got the yen probably not much different in a year's time from where it is now." \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading around 119.19 at 1021 GMT on Wednesday and has been undermined after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's revelation last week that it had covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials worry that a strong yen could hurt Japan's exports, one of the few bright spots in an economy that some say is close to entering its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it instructed the Bank of Japan to buy some $6 billion to weaken the yen and Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa added the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a warning due to recent turbulent moves. \par \par \uc2  Some in the market have said the yen could have risen to 110 yen or even 100 yen to the dollar if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  NEW GOVERNOR \par \par \uc2  Forecasts for the dollar versus yen ranged from 108.00 to 140.00 on a 12-month view. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and threats of further action have put a floor under under dollar/yen near term, although the battle to reverse the trend is likely to run into the strong headwind of repatriation," said Peter Luxton at MMS International, forecasting 127.00 in a year's time. \par \par \uc2  The selection of a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, to be announced around February 20, will also be key, Luxton said. \par \par \uc2  "The choice of BoJ governor...is also an important weather vane for monetary policy and whether radical policies will be pursued. However, probably a more mainstream candidate will be appointed, limiting the monetary effects on the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  Chris Furness at 4CAST said: "Japanese policymakers are awaiting the new bank governor...but overall there is going to be very little change in the Japanese attitude to the yen. They will want it weaker." \par \par \uc2  He added that the yen forecasts could be upset if China decided to revalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  "The only wild card that could change things dramatically is the unlikely event that China revalues the yuan...that could spoil the forecasts quite dramatically because it would take away at least one of the reasons why the Japanese authorities want to try keep the yen weak." \par \par \uc2  Countries such as Japan and South Korea have called on China to revalue the yuan to better reflect the country's competitiveness, although China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vowed last week to keep the yuan stable. \par \par \uc2  Implicit cross rates from the poll showed euro/yen at 129.60 in one month's time, 129.38 in three, 128.52 in six and 129.80 in 12 months, according to the mid-range forecast. \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030205dz2500op4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
