\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar plumbs 4-year low vs euro, focus on Powell. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2003\par 09:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to four-year lows against the euro and the Swiss franc on Wednesday ahead of U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell's attempt to persuade the world that war with Iraq is justified. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that dollar sentiment remained bearish ahead of Powell's address before the U.N. Security Council at 1530 GMT, when he will reveal what Washington says is evidence that Iraq is hiding banned weapons of mass destruction and obstructing U.N. arms inspectors. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is trading defensively ahead of comments from Powell this afternoon," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  "We have had quite a significant decline in the run-up to the speech." \par \par \uc2  At 0835 GMT the euro was trading at $1.0935 , nearly half a percent above the U.S. close and at the session's peak , the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strongest level since March 1999. The euro broke through key resistance around $1.0910 to reach these latest highs. \par \par \uc2  The euro's rise against the dollar pushed the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 3-1/2-year high of 130.54 yen . \par \par \uc2  The safe-haven Swiss franc hit a four-year peak of 1.3410  against the dollar, nearly a half percent above late New York levels. \par \par \uc2  The official release of German unemployment data is due around 0855 GMT, after a source told Reuters on Tuesday that German unemployment hit a five-year high in January, with the number of jobless climbing by 398,000 to an unadjusted 4.623 million. Unemployment rose by a higher than expected 62,000 in January on a seasonally adjusted basis. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters PMI services sector survey for January is due at 0900 GMT, and is forecast to give a reading of 50.5, down from the previous 50.6. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 119.31 yen , a third of a percent below the previous day's close and 1-1/2 yen below six-week highs set on Monday. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hit its lowest in more than three years against an index of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  . \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow testifies before the House Budget Committee at 1500 GMT and the Senate Finance Committee at 1900 GMT on President Bush's 2004 budget. \par \par \uc2  Gold prices  rose to levels not seen in more than six years as investors continued to seek safe havens. Oil prices also gained more than two percent on supply concerns in the event of war. \par \par \uc2  "Gold, short-term U.S. Treasuries and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are being bought and there's only one reason: everybody is conscious of a possible war," said Kota Kimura, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  WAR DAMAGE \par \par \uc2  The market's focus is on whether Washington will be able to persuade sceptical allies such as France, Germany and Russia that an attack against Iraq is crucial for the international community. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush hinted last week that the United States could attack Iraq even without a U.N. resolution. Traders think the dollar will be damaged if Bush chooses to do so. \par \par \uc2  Investors worry that a long-lasting war would jeopardise the fragile U.S. economic recovery, trigger a spike in oil prices and put the brakes on corporate profit growth. \par \par \uc2  The bulk of the fourth-quarter reporting season is over with few signs that U.S. corporate earnings are poised for a solid upturn in 2003. \par \par \uc2  Weakness on Wall Street discourages fund inflows to the United States from the rest of the world and burdens the dollar. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks fell by more than one percent on Tuesday and index futures were indicating a softer open on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Still, traders said the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep the dollar supported, at least against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry revealed last week that the Bank of Japan had quietly sold yen for dollars in the second half of January. Japan is worried that a strong yen is hurting exports and could derail a faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030205dz2500gzk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
