\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar near multi-year lows, market braces for Powell. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2003\par 03:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The dollar stuck near multi-year lows against the euro and the Swiss franc in Asia on Wednesday morning ahead of a much-awaited address by U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell to the U.N Security Council. \par \par \uc2  "Gold, short-term U.S. Treasuries and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are being bought and there's only one reason. Everybody is conscious of a possible war," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Gold prices rose to levels not seen in more than six years as investors continued to seek safe havens. Oil prices also rose more than two percent. \par \par \uc2  As of 0215 GMT, the euro was steady at $1.0877/82 little changed from $1.0890 in late Tuesday U.S. trade and not far from a three-year peak of 1.0910 hit in New York on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 119.71/74 yen compared to 119.68 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The safe-haven Swiss franc was at 1.3462/67 per dollar flat from late U.S. levels and just shy of a four-year peak of 1.3435 francs per dollar set on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The market was subdued as wariness prevailed ahead of Powell's presentation of what the United States says is evidence that Iraq is hiding banned weapons of mass destruction and obstructing U.N. arms inspectors. \par \par \uc2  The U.N. session, scheduled to begin at 1530 GMT, is expected to be the best opportunity for Washington to win over sceptical allies. \par \par \uc2  POWELL UNDER SPOTLIGHT \par \par \uc2  Dealers are keen to see if Powell can impress just as the United States did in a similar situation during the 1962 Cuban missile crisis, or whether he will fail to convince other nations to support the U.S. line. \par \par \uc2  "It's not yet clear if the U.S will go to war after seeking another resolution by the U.N Security Council," said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I think the euro will remain favoured. It will reach $1.10 sooner or later," Ueno predicted. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush hinted last week that the U.S. could attack Iraq even without a U.N resolution. Traders think the dollar will be damaged if Bush chooses to do so. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, U.S. stock prices also succumbed to war fears, on top of news that insurance giant American International Group will take a huge charge, with the Dow falling 1.2 percent and the Nasdaq dropping 1.3 percent \par \par \uc2  Investors worry that war would jeopardise the United States' fragile economic recovery, trigger a spike in oil prices and put the brakes on corporate profit growth. \par \par \uc2  The bulk of the fourth-quarter reporting season is over with few signs that corporate earnings are poised for a solid upturn in 2003. \par \par \uc2  Weakness on Wall Street discourages fund inflows to the Unites States from the rest of the world and burdens the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Still, traders said the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep the dollar supported, at least against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  revealed last week that the Bank of Japan had quietly sold yen for dollars in the second half of January. Japan is worried that a strong yen hurts exports and could derail a faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also faring poorly against the euro, which hit a new 3 = year high of 130.45 yen overnight. It last traded at 130.15/27 yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030205dz25004sb\par }\page {\par\fs20{