\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Advances Sharply Against Euro, Ends Higher vs. Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 951 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2003\par 00:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Having slid to fresh multiyear lows early in  the day, the dollar performed an about-face Wednesday, taking heart from a sense that a closely-watched speech by U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell at the United Nations should garner broader support for a war on Iraq, reducing the threat of unilateral U.S. action. \par \par \uc2  In a wide ranging presentation to the Security Council, Mr. Powell methodically made his case, arguing that Iraq has  defied all demands that it disarm. He presented tape recordings, satellite photos and informants' statements that  he said constituted "irrefutable and undeniable" evidence that Iraqi President Saddam Hussein is concealing weapons of  mass destruction. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Powell also accused Iraq of previously offering al Qaeda operatives training in the production and use of weapons of mass destruction -- and of harboring al Qaeda associates now. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the euro was trading at $1.0795, sharply down from an intraday high of $1.0935 and from $1.0890 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was changing hands at 120 yen, up from 119.66 yen Tuesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was changing hands at 1.3588 Swiss francs, up from a session low of 1.3408 francs and also up from 1.3456 francs late Tuesday in New York. The  pound was valued at $1.6418, down from $1.6427 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Powell's appearance has been widely signaled as a critical event for U.S. asset markets, to which traders paid  close attention, with many anticipating that it would prove  somewhat of a watershed event for the dollar. After the dollar jumped higher in several stages during Mr. Powell's speech, it traded skittishly in swift response to the reaction of other U.N. members. \par \par \uc2  Still, the overall direction for the greenback in response to the day's events was higher, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market appeared convinced that the secretary of state's presentation would convince U.S. allies that a firmer stance  toward Iraq is necessary, reducing the chance of unilateral  action by the U.S. and U.K. \par \par \uc2  "Perhaps the market is thinking this evidence will persuade others to back the U.S." in its resolve to see Iraq  disarmed by any means necessary, said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  Signs that the U.S. is more likely to act alone to bring  Iraq into compliance have in recent months tended to weaken  the dollar and raise questions about the outlook for broader U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Powell's speech helped the dollar to rebound from weakness earlier in the day, when it had slumped to fresh four-year lows against the Swiss franc and 46-month lows against the euro, although the dollar ended off its session highs. \par \par \uc2  Gold -- a traditional safe haven asset in times of war -- was well off Wednesday's earlier global session highs and  fresh six-year highs above $382 per ounce, at $372.25 per ounce. \par \par \uc2  The Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars -- which tend to benefit when gold is rallying -- all ended the  day weaker. While they have benefited in recent weeks from various factors including favorable interest-rate differentials, the dollar's generalized weakness since December has been a key factor behind their appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the Australian dollar was trading at 58.96 U.S. cents, down from 59.11 cents late Tuesday, while the New Zealand dollar was worth 54.74 cents,  down from 54.87 cents. The dollar was valued at $1.5206 Canadian, up from $1.5144 Canadian Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's inability to hold onto its strongest level of the day may have prevented it from enjoying the technically-driven upside it would have otherwise enjoyed against the euro, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  If the euro had closed Wednesday below the $1.0770 area,  "that would be particularly damaging" on a technical basis,  and could have signaled the end to the dollar's downward trajectory versus the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has held true since December, said Andrew Chaveriat, a technical \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  As has been the case in recent days, the dollar remained  the most stable against the yen, as fears of more covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to keep the greenback within narrow ranges against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Last week, the Japanese government said it quietly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January by as much as 700 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  Amid all the focus on Iraq, economic indicators took a back seat, even as they proved more favorable to the U.S. than the euro zone. The euro-zone purchasing managers' services index fell to 50.2 in January from 50.6 in December, while Germany's unemployment rate rose to 11.1% in  January, up from 10.1% in December. \par \par \uc2  Activity in the U.S. service sector grew at a slightly stronger pace in January than in December, with the Institute for Supply Management reporting that its nonmanufacturing index for the first month of the year rose slightly more than expected, to 54.5 from 54.2 in December. \par \par \uc2  On the monetary-policy front, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England will each announce the results of their latest policy meetings Thursday. A majority of economists expect that both banks will keep their respective  interest rates on hold for now. \par \par \uc2  -- John Parry in New York contributed to this article. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  02-05-03 1808ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030205dz2500aht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
