\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar limps as market awaits Powell speech. \b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2003\par 00:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The dollar remained on the backfoot in early Asian trade on Wednesday after sharp falls overnight ahead of events at the United Nations that could bring the United States and its allies closer to war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell is to present evidence to the Security Council later in the day that Iraq is hiding banned weapons of mass destruction and obstructing U.N. arms inspectors. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the presentation will drum up international support for military action against Baghdad is unnerving markets. \par \par \uc2  Gold prices rose to levels not seen in more than six years as investors continued to seek safe havens. Oil prices rose more than two percent. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock prices, already burdened by news that insurance giant American International Group will take a big charge, also succumbed to war fears, with the Dow falling 1.2 percent and the Nasdaq dropping 1.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  "There's just a general feeling of uncertainty before the Powell speech," said a trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  At 2309 GMT the dollar traded at 119.63/68 yen against 119.65 in late New York and far from a 1 = month high of 120.93 seen earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  The euro kept its edge on the dollar, supported near late New York levels at $1.0885/90 after hitting a new three-year high of $1.0910 overnight. \par \par \uc2  While economists say a short war with Iraq would have little if any effect on U.S. growth, the possibility of a drawn-out conflict or a new wave of attacks against U.S. interests in the event of war has hurt consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  It has also caused businesses to delay new investment and discouraged investors from buying U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  PUT TO HALT \par \par \uc2  The dollar's losses on Tuesday put a halt to what was starting to look like a correction of the greenback's losses. \par \par \uc2  "We had some distractions last week, like action from monetary authorities, but geopolitical factors have remained unchanged," said a trader at a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  Still, traders said the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep the dollar supported, at least against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  revealed last week that the Bank of Japan had quietly sold yen for dollars in the second half of January. Japan is worried that a strong yen hurts exports and could derail a faltering economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also faring poorly against the euro, which hit a new 3 = year high of 130.45 yen overnight. It last traded at 130.25/32 yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...        Debt...      Indicators...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par  Official rates...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......             Top events........\par  Diaries...........        Diaries Index........\par  Press Digests....         Polls on G7 economies..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030204dz2401982\par }\pa