\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls heavily as market awaits Powell report. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2003\par 22:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 4 (Reuters) - The dollar fell sharply against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday as the market worried about war the day before the United States was to present evidence against Iraq to the United Nations. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar fell more than 1 percent against the euro and the Swiss\par franc, touching a new three-year low against the euro at $1.0908 per\par euro  and a fresh four-year low versus the Swiss franc at 1.3437 francs\par       .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell on Wednesday will reveal evidence that the United States says proves that Iraqi leader Saddam Hussein has been hiding weapons of mass destruction and standing in the way of U.N. arms inspectors. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that Powell's comments could bring the United States and its allies closer to war with Iraq unnerved markets on Tuesday, even though U.S. President George W. Bush announced a week ago that Powell would make such a report. \par \par \uc2  "How many times have we heard that the war risk premium is already in the market?" asked Grant Wilson, vice president at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. "But the market is still beating the same drum. Any sign of geopolitical risk, and it's a good enough reason to sell the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Gold prices rose to levels not seen in more than six years as investors continued to seek safe investment havens and oil prices rose more than 2 percent. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock prices, already burdened by news insurance giant American International Group (AIG.N) would take a big charge, also succumbed to war fears. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average  fell 1.2 percent while the Nasdaq     dropped 1.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The world is fixating on tomorrow's visit by Powell to the U.N. Security Council," said Michael McGuinness, head of North American sales at American Express Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  "If Mr. Powell gives a credible report and the Council agrees that Iraq is in breach of previously outlined U.N. resolutions, then the Council may give its support to a coalition to eradicate any and all weapons of mass destruction from Iraq," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been under pressure for more than a year, but losses accelerated late in 2002 on the threat of war. The dollar has fallen more than 8 percent against the euro since early December of last year. \par \par \uc2  While economists say a short war with Iraq would have little if any effect on U.S. growth, the possibility of a drawn-out conflict or a new wave of attacks against U.S. interests in the event of war has hurt consumer sentiment, caused businesses to delay new investment and discouraged investors from buying U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  By late afternoon in New York, the dollar was off its lows but still down sharply on the day, trading at $1.0085 per euro and at 1.3459 Swiss francs. \par \par \uc2  SO MUCH FOR THE DOLLAR CORRECTION \par \par \uc2  The dollar's losses on Tuesday put a halt to what was starting to look like a promising correction of the greenback's huge losses. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the dollar pushed up against heavy technical resistance at $1.0680-90 per euro but failed to breach it. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar bulls had their opportunity and couldn't follow through, so the bear trend is intact," said Jordan Kotick, technical analyst at JP Morgan Chase in New York. "But we still have to be cautiously bullish on the euro, keeping in mind that this is an extended trend and there should be a substantial short-term correction at some point," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell about a half percent against the yen to 119.65 yen . But dealers said the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the dollar supported. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan revealed last week that it had quietly sold yen for dollars in the second half of January. Japan is worried about the damage to exports of a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  "There were good bids for dollar/yen all the way down, raising speculation about whether there was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," said Mellon Bank's Wilson. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/yen is still bid though. It's easier to go long euros against the yen knowing (Japan's) \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is behind you," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at 130.29 yen  in late New York trade, up 0.5 percent on the day. It rose as high as 130.47 yen earlier on Tuesday, a new 3-1/2 year high. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030204dz24016af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
