\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar backpedals, euro takes advantage. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2003\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 4 (Reuters) - The dollar slid back within a cent of last week's three-year low against the euro on Tuesday as nervousness ahead of a keynote speech by U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell thwarted the greenback's recent recovery. \par \par \uc2  With the yen still flagging after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's revelation last week that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro was the best performer rising to 130 yen, its highest since May 1999. \par \par \uc2  Powell will present the U.S. case that Iraq is hiding banned weapons from arms inspectors to the U.N. Security Council on Wednesday. His evidence may be crucial in determining whether Washington can drum up support from wary allies for a military strike against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "Powell's presentation tomorrow means Iraq is firmly on the radar screen and the risks to the dollar are still to the downside," said Jim McCormick, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  A unilateral attack by the United States is expected to bode ill for the dollar, which has already shed a fifth of its value against the euro in the past year. \par \par \uc2  By 1310 GMT, the dollar was a third of a percent lower at $1.0818 per euro and nearly a quarter-percent weaker at 120.05 yen. \par \par \uc2  EURO STRONG \par \par \uc2  The euro made most of its gains in Asian trade and gave little reaction to data showing euro zone inflation fell more than expected to 2.1 percent last month. \par \par \uc2  Both the European Commission and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have said they expect inflation to start coming down this year, a process that will be helped the euro's recent rise on the foreign exchanges. \par \par \uc2  "The market is so blinkered it is not really interested in euro zone fundamentals," said Mark Henry, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at GNI. "War concerns are dominating and people lack the conviction to buy into any dollar recovery, even though the euro is looking overbought." \par \par \uc2  After recent comments from euro zone policymakers welcoming the euro's strength although perhaps not its speed, Greek \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member Nicholas Garganas called the gain long overdue. \par \par \uc2  He said it was a move in the right direction to correct world imbalances and dismissed the idea of attempting to stabilise the market through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "futile". \par \par \uc2  The yen, meanwhile, remained limp as dealers digested comments from Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa that last month's covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a "warning measure". \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi chimed in, saying he was watching closely for sharp fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials worry that a strong yen could hurt the country's exports, one of the few bright spots in an economy that some say is close to entering its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  U.S. POLICY FOCUS \par \par \uc2  With little major U.S. economic data to give direction, dealers said war fears, Wall Street and U.S. policy questions would dominate. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock market futures were pointing to a weak open on Wall Street as geopolitical worries returned to the fore and technology bellwther Cisco eases in pre-trade ahead of an expected bleak forecast. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow is due to testify before the tax-writing House Ways and Means Committee later on Tuesday and will testify twice on Wednesday, firstly before the House Budget Committee and then the Senate Finance Committee. \par \par \uc2  Snow is expected to face tough questioning from lawmakers on the budget proposals which project deficits of $304 billion for the current fiscal year and $307 billion next year, surpassing a 1992 record of $290 billion. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030204dz2400qeh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
