\par\par \b \uc2  JGB futures up amid confusion over next BOJ boss. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2003\par 07:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Key 10-year Japanese government bond (JGB) futures held onto gains on Monday after Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi quickly denied a report he would put inflation-targeting proponent Nobuyuki Nakahara in charge of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  A policy targeting inflation would be negative for the JGB market as higher interest rates would cause capital losses to Japanese financial institutions, which have aggressively boosted their JGB portfolios in recent years, dealers and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The market was also concerned that fiscal spending would increase if Nakahara were to get the job. \par \par \uc2  "Nakahara as the new BOJ governor would be a JGB-selling factor as an inflation-targeting policy would be achieved by further monetary easing by the BOJ, plus more and more fiscal spending by the government," said Noboru Iwamatsu, senior manager at Mizuho Bank. \par \par \uc2  Nakahara said in an interview in Sunday's edition of the Nihon Keizai Shimbun (Nikkei) financial daily that the government should increase fiscal spending, while the BOJ should help the government's funding by buying more JGBs and adopting an inflation target. \par \par \uc2  He said the government should pump out as much as 10 trillion yen ($83 billion) in fiscal spending and tax cuts over three years, and the BOJ should aim for an inflation rate of three percent. \par \par \uc2  More outright JGB purchasing by the BOJ to achieve an inflation target could be a positive for JGBs, but market participants are more worried about a possible deterioration in the BOJ's asset quality and its credibility, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has been saying it will keep its easy policy by buying long-term JGBs outright until the consumer price index (CPI) rises above zero, which is effectively an inflation-targeting policy already," said Hiroyuki Kubota, bond markets director at Research and Pricing Technologies Inc. \par \par \uc2  "The inflation-targeting policy currently being discussed involves measures other than outright JGB purchases, such as buying stocks, real estate and foreign bonds, and the market fears that these measures would significantly hurt the BOJ's asset quality." \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ decided to adopt such measures, Kubota said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also lose credibility and cause a rise in interest rates based on the government's fiscal position and not the economy's strength. \par \par \uc2  The key 10-year JGB contract for March delivery  ended the day-session up 0.04 point at 142.83. It rose as high as 143.01 after Koizumi denied the report. \par \par \uc2  Both the news wire report and Koizumi's denial came while the market was closed for the midsession break. \par \par \uc2  Cash bond prices edged down as investors adjusted positions to make room for new 10-year bonds to be offered on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the benchmark 245th 10-year cash bond  was up one basis point at 0.810 percent. \par \par \uc2  AUCTION \par \par \uc2  Market participants appeared generally optimistic about Tuesday's auction of 1.8 trillion yen ($15 billion) in 10-year government bonds even though the coupon is expected to be set at a record low of 0.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  "In recent auctions we've seen a similar pattern - that JGBs got well-bid afterwards. I think the market should follow this trend, reflecting the current bullish sentiment," said Shinichi Sato, strategist at Mitsubishi Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, the present bullish trend in the market was set after the last auction, where the bid-to-cover ratio was 18.6 times," Sato said. \par \par \uc2  Others were more cautious. \par \par \uc2  "As the new bonds will be issued on February 20, there won't be much time to earn income gains before the end of the business year (in March), especially with a coupon of 0.8 percent," said Makoto Yamashita, fixed income strategist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities. \par \par \uc2  "On top of (next week's) BOJ Policy Board meeting and the Group of Seven meeting set later in the month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also likely to make some participants hesitant to buy JGBs," he added. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the Finance Ministry confirmed that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  quietly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after mid-January to stem the yen's rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen could ease the appetite among yen-based investors, such as life insurers, to sell JGBs for the purpose of covering losses from U.S. asset investments, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  For example, if the dollar were below 118 yen, many life insurers would face \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses from U.S. dollar-based investments, they said. ($1=119.87 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030203dz2300cte\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
