\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's stealthy  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  keeps market on toes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2003\par 07:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - If a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, should it try to draw attention to itself? \par \par \uc2  Early signs are that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new tactic of covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is having at least one of its desired effects - keeping the market on its toes. \par \par \uc2  Longer-term, too, the strategy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in smaller lots and keeping quiet about it at the time is likely to prove an effective way to keep the yen in check, especially if it is combined with more traditional large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it had the Bank of Japan buy some $6 billion in dollars for yen since mid-January. \par \par \uc2  Previously, the MOF was quick to announce that it was in the market. This time, though, most traders were taken by surprise. \par \par \uc2  "Since dollar/yen is now around 120 yen from 117 or 118 in mid-January when they were seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think you can say the action did have an effect," said Motoshi Imura, senior manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "I think this is a sign that Japanese authorities are becoming more flexible to market movements." \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, confirmed on Monday that flexibility was now the MOF's watchword. \par \par \uc2  "We will take various measures to deal appropriately with market movements. By not acting as the market expects, we can raise the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  "If market movements become rapid, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a massive scale is possible," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials worry that a strong yen could hurt Japan's exports, one of the few bright spots in an economy that some say is close to entering its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  Some in the market said the yen could have risen to 110 yen or even 100 yen to the dollar if the MOF had stayed on the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  EYE ON MARCH 31 \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the MOF was particularly keen to stem the yen's rise ahead of the end of the fiscal year on March 31. Any sign that the economy was hurtling towards a recession could hit the stock market and exacerbate banks' bad loan problems. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the first since Mizoguchi took over as vice finance minister for international affairs on January 14. \par \par \uc2  Analysts estimate that Japanese authorities spent about 200 to 300 billion yen per time, at the 117 yen level. \par \par \uc2  In recent years, notably under the regimes of Eisuke Sakakibara and Haruhiko Kuroda - Mizoguchi's immediate predecessor - the MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in large amounts, which had an immediate impact on rates and which it immediately confirmed. \par \par \uc2  This time, however, the ministry kept quiet and the market hardly noticed that it had acted. \par \par \uc2  "These were unusual in that they were small and secret," said Marshall Gittler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said the Japanese authorities probably wanted to keep a low profile ahead of hearings in late January to confirm railroad executive John Snow as U.S. Treasury Secretary. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said the MOF did not actually try to conceal the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We had no such intention...it was a result. If the amounts were big, it would be visible. But we took pre-emptive steps and therefore it was invisible." \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy comes under the jurisdiction of the MOF, and is carried out by the Bank of Japan at the ministry's direction. Mizoguchi said the market had recently shown signs of moving rapidly due to geopolitical risks, and that such movements were being exacerbated by derivatives and programme trading. \par \par \uc2  Traders said, however, that the outlook for the dollar's exchange rate against the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely depended on attitudes towards the dollar, which is hostage to a possible war in Iraq and an uncertain economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar-selling trend has not changed," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at JP Morgan Chase. "But against the yen, people will get cautious if it falls greatly. \par \par \uc2  "I think 117.50 yen will become a key level," he said. \par \par \uc2  NOT AIMING FOR LOWER YEN \par \par \uc2  In a move to possibly head off criticism from other countries, Mizoguchi reiterated that any Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at preventing volatility, not at driving the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen would make it more difficult for other exporting countries, such as China and South Korea, to sell their products on international markets. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be to guide the yen lower but to prevent fluctuations," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly surged to 120.93 yen on Monday morning - its highest level since mid December - after a Kyodo news agency report said Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi was set to name inflation-targeting advocate Nobuyuki Nakahara, a former BOJ board member, to succeed outgoing governor Masaru Hayami. \par \par \uc2  Many traders say the appointment of Nakahara, an advocate of inflation targeting as well as foreign bond buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would be negative for the yen. The dollar shed its gains after Koizumi flatly denied the report. \par \par \uc2  "There is some bizarre speculation and false reports swirling today but it is still a blank sheet of paper," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was trading around 120.20 yen in early afternoon trade in Asia compared with 119.89 in late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030203dz2300bf6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
